the same directory you run it from by hand — and prompts you to trust the new server. Confirm, then run **MCP: List Servers** from the Command Palette (`Ctrl+Shift+P` / `Cmd+Shift+P`): `weather` shows a running status.\n./docs/get-started/real-host.md:68:3. VS Code — the MCP **client** inside the host — sends a `tools/call` request to your server over stdio.\n./docs/get-started/real-host.md:69:4. The SDK validates the arguments against your `inputSchema` and runs your handler.\n./docs/get-started/real-host.md:75:Every MCP host launches a stdio server from the same command and arguments. Only where you put them differs.\n./docs/get-started/real-host.md:102:The entry is the same `command` plus `args`; only the wrapper key and the file name change. The server appears under Cursor's MCP settings with `get-alerts` listed, and agent chat calls it the same way.\n./docs/get-started/real-host.md:115:weather MCP server running on stdio\n./docs/get-started/real-host.md:122:- The server runs but Copilot never calls it: confirm the chat is in **Agent** mode, then run **MCP: Reset Cached Tools** from the Command Palette.\n./test/conformance/src/helpers/withOAuthRetry.ts:29:        provider.invalidateCredentials('discovery');\n./test/conformance/src/helpers/withOAuthRetry.ts:100:                const headers = new Headers(init?.headers);\n./test/conformance/src/helpers/withOAuthRetry.ts:102:                // Add authorization header if tokens are available\n./test/conformance/src/helpers/withOAuthRetry.ts:105:                    headers.set('Authorization', `Bearer ${tokens.access_token}`);\n./test/conformance/src/helpers/withOAuthRetry.ts:108:                return await next(input, { ...init, headers });\n./typedoc.config.mjs:22:// (the schemas mirror the documented types 1:1) so monorepo-wide invalid-link validation can stay ON.\n./typedoc.config.mjs:34:    name: 'MCP TypeScript SDK (V2)',\n./test/conformance/expected-failures.yaml:38:    - tasks-request-headers\n./REVIEW.md:62:- MCP documentation server: `https://modelcontextprotocol.io/mcp`\n./REVIEW.md:70:- When validating `Mcp-Session-Id`, return **400** for a missing header and **404** for an unknown/expired session — never conflate `!sessionId || !transports[sessionId]` into one status, because the client needs to distinguish \"fix your request\" from \"start a new session\". Flag any diff that branches on session-id presence/lookup with a single 4xx. (#1707, #1770)\n./docs/_meta/CONVENTIONS.md:15:    MCP\" orientation paragraph + a spec link BEFORE the code. Every other page: code first.\n./docs/_meta/CONVENTIONS.md:23:    (\"From that one schema the SDK derives the JSON Schema the model sees, validates arguments\n./docs/_meta/CONVENTIONS.md:70:    model sees, validates arguments before your handler runs,\n./docs/_meta/CONVENTIONS.md:81:    * One Zod schema: wire schema, validation, handler types.\n./test/helpers/src/helpers/http.ts:29: * - set()/header()\n./test/helpers/src/helpers/http.ts:61:            // header value is ignored in the generic mock; tests spy on set()\n./test/helpers/src/helpers/http.ts:64:        header: vi.fn<Response['header']>().mockImplementation((_field: string, _value?: string | string[]) => {\n./docs/get-started/packages.md:57:`createMcpHandler` from `@modelcontextprotocol/server` already serves MCP over web-standard `Request` and `Response` objects. An adapter package wires that handler into a specific runtime or framework; install the adapter next to the framework it adapts.\n./docs/get-started/packages.md:63:Four adapters exist: `@modelcontextprotocol/node` for Node's built-in `http` server, and one each for Express, Hono, and Fastify. They are thin layers over `createMcpHandler` and add no MCP behavior of their own.\n./docs/get-started/packages.md:67:## Reach for `core` only to validate raw wire JSON\n./docs/get-started/packages.md:69:`@modelcontextprotocol/core` exports the Zod schema constants the SDK validates protocol payloads against, for code that handles raw JSON-RPC payloads itself — gateways, proxies, log pipelines. Neither `server` nor `client` exports a Zod schema, and the matching TypeScript types ship with both, so if you only call `registerTool` and `callTool` you never install it. [Wire schemas](../advanced/wire-schemas.md) is the how-to.\n./docs/get-started/packages.md:80:- `@modelcontextprotocol/core` exports only Zod schema constants, for code that validates raw wire JSON itself.\n./docs/protocol-versions.md:96:A probe timeout is transport-aware. On stdio a silent server is a legacy server, so `connect()` falls back to `initialize` on the same stream; on HTTP silence is an outage, so `connect()` rejects with `SdkError(RequestTimeout)` instead of misreporting a dead server as legacy. One browser exception: an opaque CORS `TypeError` during the probe falls back to the legacy era, because deployed 2025 servers commonly have allow-lists that predate the 2026 headers.\n./test/helpers/src/helpers/oauth.ts:36:                    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }\n./test/helpers/src/helpers/oauth.ts:53:                    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }\n./test/helpers/src/helpers/oauth.ts:71:                    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }\n./docs/get-started/first-client.md:7:Build an MCP **client** — the program that launches a server, lists its tools, and calls them — against the weather server from [Build your first server](./first-server.md).\n./docs/get-started/first-client.md:17:Create `src/client.ts`. A `Client` plus one transport is a complete MCP client.\n./docs/get-started/first-client.md:55:weather MCP server running on stdio\n./docs/get-started/first-client.md:81:Input validation error: Invalid arguments for tool get-alerts: state: Too big: expected string to have <=2 characters\n./docs/get-started/first-client.md:152:- A `Client` plus one transport is a complete MCP client; `connect()` runs the initialize handshake.\n./docs/get-started/first-server.md:8:Build an MCP **server** — a program that exposes tools a model can call — and call its one tool, a US weather-alert lookup, from a client.\n./docs/get-started/first-server.md:53:            const res = await fetch(url, { headers: { 'User-Agent': 'mcp-weather-tutorial/1.0' } });\n./docs/get-started/first-server.md:70:`registerTool` takes a name, a config, and an async handler. `inputSchema` is a Zod schema — the only schema you write. From that one schema the SDK derives the JSON Schema the model sees, validates arguments before your handler runs, and infers the handler's argument types.\n./docs/get-started/first-server.md:78:Input validation error: Invalid arguments for tool get-alerts: state: Too big: expected string to have <=2 characters\n./docs/get-started/first-server.md:89:console.error('weather MCP server running on stdio');\n./docs/get-started/first-server.md:109:weather MCP server running on stdio\n./docs/get-started/first-server.md:116:The **MCP Inspector** is a local web app for calling a server's tools directly — it launches the command you give it and connects over stdio.\n./docs/get-started/first-server.md:133:- The SDK validates every call against that schema and rejects bad arguments before your handler runs.\n./test/conformance/src/helpers/conformanceOAuthProvider.ts:63:    invalidateCredentials(scope: 'all' | 'client' | 'tokens' | 'verifier' | 'discovery'): void {\n./test/conformance/src/helpers/conformanceOAuthProvider.ts:76:            // Get the Location header which contains the redirect with auth code\n./test/conformance/src/helpers/conformanceOAuthProvider.ts:77:            const location = response.headers.get('location');\n./test/conformance/src/helpers/conformanceOAuthProvider.ts:81:                // RFC 9207: capture `iss` alongside `code` for validation before token exchange.\n./README.md:1:# MCP TypeScript SDK\n./README.md:5:> **This is the `main` branch — v2 of the SDK, now in beta** (`@modelcontextprotocol/server`, `@modelcontextprotocol/client`), implementing the [2026-07-28 MCP spec](https://blog.modelcontextprotocol.io/posts/2026-07-28-release-candidate/).\n./README.md:35:The Model Context Protocol (MCP) allows applications to provide context for LLMs in a standardized way, separating the concerns of providing context from the actual LLM interaction.\n./README.md:37:This repository contains the TypeScript SDK implementation of the MCP specification. It runs on **Node.js**, **Bun**, and **Deno**, and ships:\n./README.md:39:- MCP **server** libraries (tools/resources/prompts, Streamable HTTP, stdio, auth helpers)\n./README.md:40:- MCP **client** libraries (transports, high-level helpers, OAuth helpers)\n./README.md:48:- **`@modelcontextprotocol/server`**: build MCP servers\n./README.md:49:- **`@modelcontextprotocol/client`**: build MCP clients\n./README.md:55:The SDK also publishes small \"middleware\" packages under [`packages/middleware/`](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/tree/main/packages/middleware) that help you **wire MCP into a specific runtime or web framework**.\n./README.md:57:They are intentionally thin adapters: they should not introduce new MCP functionality or business logic. See [`packages/middleware/README.md`](packages/middleware/README.md) for details.\n./README.md:60:- **`@modelcontextprotocol/express`**: Express helpers (app defaults + Host header validation)\n./README.md:61:- **`@modelcontextprotocol/hono`**: Hono helpers (app defaults + JSON body parsing hook + Host header validation)\n./README.md:87:The SDK also publishes optional “middleware” packages that help you **wire MCP into a specific runtime or web framework** (for example Express, Hono, or Node.js `http`).\n./README.md:89:These packages are intentionally thin adapters and should not introduce additional MCP features or business logic. See [`packages/middleware/README.md`](packages/middleware/README.md) for details.\n./README.md:104:Here is what an MCP server looks like. This minimal example exposes a single `greet` tool over stdio:\n./README.md:143:- [Build a server](docs/get-started/first-server.md) — your first MCP server, step by step\n./README.md:144:- [Build a client](docs/get-started/first-client.md) — your first MCP client, step by step\n./README.md:148:- [MCP documentation](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs)\n./README.md:149:- [MCP specification](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/latest)\n./test/conformance/src/authTestServer.ts:4: * MCP Auth Test Server - Conformance Test Server with Authentication\n./test/conformance/src/authTestServer.ts:6: * A minimal MCP server that requires Bearer token authentication.\n./test/conformance/src/authTestServer.ts:10: * - MCP_CONFORMANCE_AUTH_SERVER_URL: URL of the authorization server\n./test/conformance/src/authTestServer.ts:34:const AUTH_SERVER_URL = process.env.MCP_CONFORMANCE_AUTH_SERVER_URL;\n./test/conformance/src/authTestServer.ts:36:    console.error('Error: MCP_CONFORMANCE_AUTH_SERVER_URL environment variable is required');\n./test/conformance/src/authTestServer.ts:37:    console.error('Usage: MCP_CONFORMANCE_AUTH_SERVER_URL=http://localhost:3000 npx tsx authTestServer.ts');\n./test/conformance/src/authTestServer.ts:52:// Function to create a new MCP server instance (one per session)\n./test/conformance/src/authTestServer.ts:125:        headers: {\n./test/conformance/src/authTestServer.ts:164:        const authHeader = req.headers.authorization;\n./test/conformance/src/authTestServer.ts:167:            res.set('WWW-Authenticate', buildWwwAuthHeader('invalid_token', 'Missing Authorization header'));\n./test/conformance/src/authTestServer.ts:170:                error_description: 'Missing Authorization header'\n./test/conformance/src/authTestServer.ts:264:    // Configure CORS to expose Mcp-Session-Id header for browser-based clients\n./test/conformance/src/authTestServer.ts:285:        const sessionId = req.headers['mcp-session-id'] as string | undefined;\n./test/co