lformed.\n./docs/serving/sessions-state-scaling.md:62:The map cleans itself up: `transport.onclose` fires when the session ends, whether the client sent `DELETE` or you called `transport.close()`. A request with an unknown `Mcp-Session-Id` gets the `404` above, which tells the client to start a new session; a request with no session header at all gets the `400`, which tells it to re-send the id it already has instead of re-initializing.\n./docs/serving/sessions-state-scaling.md:81:When the connection drops, the client reconnects with the last event id it received as a `Last-Event-ID` header and the transport replays everything stored after it. The SDK's `StreamableHTTPClientTransport` reconnects and sends that header on its own.\n./docs/serving/sessions-state-scaling.md:105:- A sessionful deployment keeps one transport per session and routes every request to it by that header; unknown ids get a `404`.\n./docs/serving/stdio.md:23:The process is now an MCP server. A host that spawns it lists and calls whatever the factory registered; until one does, the process waits on stdin.\n./docs/serving/stdio.md:53:The **MCP Inspector** launches your server command itself and connects to it over stdio.\n./test/e2e/helpers/index.ts:62:    /** The HTTP request headers as resolved by `new Request(...)` — for raw header assertions (e.g. `Mcp-Param-*`). */\n./test/e2e/helpers/index.ts:68:    /** Response content-type header (empty string when absent). */\n./test/e2e/helpers/index.ts:89: * wire-sniffer.ts): every message the client sends or receives is validated\n./test/e2e/helpers/index.ts:92: * deliberately put malformed MCP on the wire pass `{ strictValidation: false }`.\n./test/e2e/helpers/index.ts:166:                    requestHeaders: request.headers,\n./test/e2e/helpers/index.ts:169:                    contentType: response.headers.get('content-type') ?? '',\n./test/e2e/helpers/index.ts:243:            const sid = req.headers.get('mcp-session-id') ?? undefined;\n./test/e2e/helpers/index.ts:255:                    { status: 400, headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' } }\n./test/e2e/helpers/index.ts:284:            const sid = req.headers.get('mcp-session-id') ?? undefined;\n./test/e2e/helpers/index.ts:318:                        headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:8: * The scenario name is read from the MCP_CONFORMANCE_SCENARIO environment variable,\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:37: * Schema for client conformance test context passed via MCP_CONFORMANCE_CONTEXT.\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:80: * Parse the conformance context from MCP_CONFORMANCE_CONTEXT env var.\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:83:    const raw = process.env.MCP_CONFORMANCE_CONTEXT;\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:85:        throw new Error('MCP_CONFORMANCE_CONTEXT not set');\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:116: * MCP_CONFORMANCE_PROTOCOL_VERSION environment variable; when it names a\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:123:    const version = process.env.MCP_CONFORMANCE_PROTOCOL_VERSION;\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:137:    logger.debug('Successfully connected to MCP server');\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:157:    logger.debug('Successfully connected to MCP server');\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:203:// MCP-Protocol-Version header and the per-request _meta envelope, and the\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:228:// SEP-2243 standard-header client scenario (Mcp-Method / Mcp-Name)\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:231:// http-standard-headers: the referee mock answers initialize, tools/list,\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:233:// asserts that each POST carried the correct Mcp-Method header (and Mcp-Name\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:234:// for the call/read/get verbs). The SDK emits both headers on the modern\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:241:// baseline bug. The mock advertises its own surface (test_headers /\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:246:    logger.debug('Successfully connected to MCP server');\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:270:registerScenario('http-standard-headers', runHttpStandardHeadersClient);\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:273:// SEP-2243 custom-header client scenarios (protocol revision 2026-07-28)\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:276:// The SEP-2243 conformance mocks (http-custom-headers / http-invalid-tool-headers)\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:281:// tool calls to make via MCP_CONFORMANCE_CONTEXT.\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:284:    const raw = process.env.MCP_CONFORMANCE_CONTEXT;\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:299:// http-custom-headers: the conformance mock advertises test_custom_headers and\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:300:// test_custom_headers_null with x-mcp-header annotations. List first (so the\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:302:// calls; the conformance mock validates the Mcp-Param-* headers it receives.\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:314:// http-invalid-tool-headers: the conformance mock advertises one valid tool\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:332:registerScenario('http-custom-headers', runHttpCustomHeadersClient);\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:333:registerScenario('http-invalid-tool-headers', runHttpInvalidToolHeadersClient);\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:372:                        { status: 200, headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' } }\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:451:    logger.debug('Successfully connected to MCP server');\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:475:        // `resource` does not match the MCP server URL; the SDK's discovery path\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:490:        // `auth()`; the SDK validates internally. Positive scenarios proceed to\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:588: * additionally validates `requested_token_type=id-jag`, ID-JAG `typ` and\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:703:    logger.debug('Successfully connected to MCP server');\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:742:    logger.debug('Successfully connected to MCP server');\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:791:    logger.debug('Successfully connected to MCP server');\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:810:    const scenarioName = process.env.MCP_CONFORMANCE_SCENARIO;\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:814:        logger.error('Usage: MCP_CONFORMANCE_SCENARIO=<scenario> everything-client <server-url>');\n./test/conformance/src/everythingClient.ts:815:        logger.error('\\nThe MCP_CONFORMANCE_SCENARIO env var is set automatically by the conformance runner.');\n./docs/serving/express.md:33:`app` is an ordinary Express app with one route — `/mcp` answers every MCP request — and nothing is listening yet. The factory runs once per request, so a fresh `McpServer` serves every call: [Serve over HTTP](./http.md#understand-the-per-request-factory) covers that model.\n./docs/serving/express.md:37:A malicious page can DNS-rebind its own domain to `127.0.0.1` and reach a localhost server as if it were same-origin. `createMcpExpressApp` validates the `Host` and `Origin` headers against that: with the default `127.0.0.1` bind (and `localhost` / `::1`), a request carrying a non-localhost value gets `403` before your handler runs.\n./docs/serving/express.md:45:`allowedHosts` and `allowedOrigins` take hostnames, port-agnostic. A request without an `Origin` header always passes, so MCP clients outside a browser are unaffected.\n./docs/serving/express.md:89:- The default `127.0.0.1` bind validates `Host` and `Origin`; pass `allowedHosts` when binding to `0.0.0.0`.\n./test/e2e/helpers/wire-sniffer.ts:113:    if (isJSONRPCErrorResponse(msg)) return; // envelope already validated; error bodies are not method-specific\n./test/e2e/helpers/wire-sniffer.ts:117: * Wrap a transport so every outbound `send` (validated as `party`) and inbound\n./test/e2e/helpers/wire-sniffer.ts:118: * `onmessage` (validated as the counterpart) is asserted. Returns the same\n./docs/serving/hono.md:42:A malicious page can DNS-rebind its own domain to `127.0.0.1` and reach a localhost server as if it were same-origin. `createMcpHonoApp` validates the `Host` and `Origin` headers against that: with the default `127.0.0.1` bind (and `localhost` / `::1`), a request carrying a non-localhost value gets `403` before your handler runs.\n./docs/serving/hono.md:50:`allowedHosts` and `allowedOrigins` take hostnames, port-agnostic. A request without an `Origin` header always passes, so MCP clients outside a browser are unaffected.\n./docs/serving/hono.md:88:- The default `127.0.0.1` bind validates `Host` and `Origin`; pass `allowedHosts` when binding to `0.0.0.0`.\n./docs/servers/tools.md:43:From that one schema the SDK derives the JSON Schema the model sees, validates arguments before your handler runs, and infers the handler's argument types.\n./docs/servers/tools.md:57:Every call on this page comes from an in-memory `Client` connected to the server above — [Test a server](../testing.md) shows that wiring — and an MCP host does the same over stdio or HTTP. Call the tool with valid arguments.\n./docs/servers/tools.md:86:      text: 'Input validation error: Invalid arguments for tool search: limit: Too big: expected number to be <=50'\n./docs/servers/tools.md:119:The SDK validates `structuredContent` against `outputSchema` before the result leaves your server, and advertises the derived JSON Schema in `tools/list` so clients can validate it too.\n./docs/servers/tools.md:156:- The one schema yields the advertised JSON Schema, argument validation, and the handler's argument types.\n./docs/servers/tools.md:158:- `outputSchema` plus `structuredContent` add machine-readable results, validated before they leave the server.\n./docs/serving/web-standard.md:29:The deployed worker answers MCP requests on every path, with no Node adapter and no body middleware. The factory runs once per request, so a fresh `McpServer` serves every call: [Serve over HTTP](./http.md#understand-the-per-request-factory) covers that model.\n./docs/serving/web-standard.md:33:The handler performs no `Host` or `Origin` validation, and on a bare fetch-native runtime there is no app factory to arm it for you. Put the framework-agnostic response helpers in front of `fetch`.\n./docs/serving/web-standard.md:47:A request whose `Host` is not on the list gets `403` before `handler.fetch` runs; both helpers take hostnames, port-agnostic, and a request without an `Origin` header always passes. For a localhost-only process, `localhostAllowedHostnames()` and `localhostAllowedOrigins()` (same package) replace the explicit lists.\n./docs/serving/web-standard.md:51:There is no body middleware on a fetch-native runtime — `fetch` reads the `Request` itself, so there is no `parsedBody` to forward. The handler never derives auth from request headers either: verify the token yourself and pass the result as `fetch`'s second argument, and handlers read it as `ctx.http.authInfo`.\n./docs/serving/web-standard.md:87:- The handler does no `Host`/`Origin` validation; on a bare runtime, put `hostHeaderValidationResponse` and `originValidationResponse` in front of it.\n./docs/serving/http.md:7:To host one MCP endpoint that many clients connect to, serve your factory over **Streamable HTTP**. A host that launches the server as a local child process speaks [stdio](./stdio.md) instead.\n./docs/serving/http.md:73:const validateHost = localhostHostValidation();\n./docs/serving/http.md:74:const validateOrigin = localhostOriginValidation();\n./docs/serving/http.md:76:    if (!validateHost(req, res) || !validateOrigin(req, res)) return;\n./docs/serving/http.md:81:`POST http://127.0.0.1:3000/mcp` now reaches the factory; the guards answer anything else with `403` before the handler sees it — [the next section](#validate-host-and-origin-in-front-of-it) explains why they belong in front. The same wrapped handler mounts under [Express](./express.md), [Fastify](./fastify.md), and [Hono](./hono.md); [Serve on web-standard runtimes](./web-standard.md) covers the `export default` side.\n./docs/serving/http.md:85:The handler trusts its caller: it validates no `Host` header, no `Origin` header, and no token. Mount those checks in front of it — on a localhost bind, the `Host` check is what stops **DNS rebinding**, a malicious page resolving its own domain to `127.0.0.1` so the browser treats your local server as same-origin.\n./docs/serving/http.md:91:`authInfo` is pass-through: the handler never reads it from headers and never verifies a token. Verify the bearer token in front of the handler and hand it the result as `fetch`'s second argument, `handler.fetch(request, { authInfo })`; the factory reads it back as `authInfo`, and tool handlers as `ctx.http.authInfo`.\n./docs/serving/http.md:133:- The handler validates no `Host` or `Origin` header and verifies no token — mount both checks in front of it; the framework app factories arm the header checks for you.\n./test/e2e/helpers/sse-host.ts:47:            // connect() starts the transport, which writes the SSE headers and the endpoint event.\n./test/e2e/helpers/sse-host.ts:66:            if (!res.headersSent) res.writeHead(500).end(toError(error).message);\n./docs/serving/authorization.md:12:Your MCP server is an OAuth **resource server**: it verifies access tokens that an authorization server issued, and it never issues them. `requireBearerAuth` from `@modelcontextprotocol/express` is that whole gate — build it from a verifier and mount it in front of the `/mcp` route from the [Express recipe](./express.md).\n./docs/serving/authorization.md:141:- `requireBearerAuth` plus a `verifyAccessToken` you write turn an Express-mounted MCP route into an OAuth resource server; the SDK never issues tokens.\n./docs/servers/resources.md:63:Every call on this page comes from an in-memory `Client` connected to the server above — [Test a server](../testing.md) shows that wiring — and an MCP host does the same over stdio or HTTP. Read the resource.\n./docs/servers/prompts.md:48:Every call on this page comes from an in-memory `Client` connected to the server above — [Test a server](../testing.md) shows that wiring — and an MCP host does the same when someone picks the prompt. Fetch it with `getPrompt`.\n./docs/servers/prompts.md:87:A failed prompt validation is a protocol error — `getPrompt` rejects with a `ProtocolError` carrying code `-32602` (Invalid params). A [tool](./tools.md) argument rejection comes back as an `isError: true` result instead.\n./docs/servers/prompts.md:89:From that one schema the SDK derives the argument list `prompts/list` advertises, validates `prompts/get` arguments before your callback runs, and infers the callback's argument types.\n./docs/servers/prompts.md:201:- `argsSchema` is one Zod object: the advertised argument list, argument validation, and the callback's argument types.\n./docs/servers/errors.md:98:A throw and an explicit `isError: true` produce the same shape; returning explicitly gives you control over `content`. The SDK skips `outputSchema` validation on any `isError` result.\n./docs/servers/errors.md:142:On the wire this is a JSON-RPC error response — `{ code, message, data? }` instead of a `result` — and the host's MCP client handles it; the model never sees it. A non-`ProtocolError` exception thrown from one of these callbacks surfaces as `-32603` Internal Error with the exception's message.\n./docs/servers/completion.md:10:`completable` wraps one field of a [prompt's](./prompts.md) `argsSchema` — the schema validates exactly as before, and the second argument suggests values for the field.\n./docs/servers/completion.md:159:A host issues the same request as the end user types: MCP Inspector requests completions while you fill in a prompt's arguments and shows the returned `values` as suggestions.\n./docs/servers/completion.md:164:- `completable(schema, callback)` attaches autocompletion to one prompt argument; the schema validates exactly as before.\n./packages/core-internal/src/errors/sdkErrors.ts:31:    /** Response result failed local schema validation */\n./docs/index.md:5:# MCP TypeScript SDK\n./docs/index.md:11:The **Model Context Protocol** (MCP) is an open standard that connects AI applications to the systems where your data and tools live. You write a **server** that exposes tools, resources, and prompts; any MCP **host** — Claude Code, VS Code, Cursor, your own application — connects to it and lets a model use them. The protocol is defined by [the MCP specification](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/latest); this SDK is its TypeScript implementation, on Node.js, Bun, and Deno.\n./docs/index.md:38:Any MCP host that launches this program lists and calls `get-forecast`; the SDK validates every call against that `z.object(...)` schema before your handler runs. [Build a server](./get-started/first-server.md) installs the packages and runs it end to end.\n./docs/index.md:43:- Build an application that talks to MCP servers → **[Build a client](./get-started/first-client.md)**\n./docs/index.md:45:- Drop MCP into the app you already run → **[Express](./serving/express.md)** · **[Hono](./serving/hono.md)** · **[Fastify](./serving/fastify.md)** · **[Workers](./serving/web-standard.md)**\n./docs/index.md:51:- MCP connects AI applications to the systems where your tools and data live; you build one side, a host brings the model.\n./docs/advanced/low-level-server.md:76:From one Zod `inputSchema` the SDK derives the JSON Schema the model sees, validates arguments before your handler runs, and infers the handler's argument types. Here you wrote the JSON Schema by hand, the cast went unchecked, and nothing tied the two together.\n./docs/advanced/low-level-server.md:80:```ts source=\"../../examples/guides/advanced/low-level-server.examples.ts#lowLevel_validate\"\n./docs/advanced/low-level-server.md:91:    const parsed = await SearchArguments['~standard'].validate(request.params.arguments ?? {});\n./docs/advanced/low-level-server.md:109:Keeping the schema you advertise in `tools/list` identical to the one you validate with is still on you — `registerTool` derives both from the same object.\n./docs/advanced/low-level-server.md:149:Default to `McpServer`. `registerTool`, `registerResource`, and `registerPrompt` cover everything this page rebuilt — schema derivation, argument validation, typed handler arguments — plus the bookkeeping it skipped: `listChanged` notifications, [completions](../servers/completion.md), and the list/read/get dispatch for every registry.\n./docs/advanced/low-level-server.md:158:- On `Server` you write the JSON Schema in `tools/list` and the argument validation in `tools/call`; `registerTool` derives both from one Zod schema.\n./docs/servers/input-required.md:53:`ctx.mcpReq.inputResponses` comes from the client — treat it as untrusted. Pass a Zod schema as `acceptedContent`'s third argument and the value reaches your handler already validated and typed.\n./docs/servers/input-required.md:248:- `acceptedContent(ctx.mcpReq.inputResponses, key, schema)` validates the untrusted client answer before it reaches your code; `inputResponse` discriminates declines and the non-elicitation kinds.\n./docs/servers/elicitation.md:45:`result.action` records what the end user did — `accept`, `decline`, or `cancel` — and `result.content` carries the submitted fields on accept only. The SDK validates accepted content against `requestedSchema` before `elicitInput` resolves, so the fields you read match the schema you sent.\n./docs/servers/elicitation.md:152:The client opens the URL and answers once the end user finishes there; whatever the page collects — credentials, payment details, API keys — stays in the browser and never crosses the MCP connection. The handler's `url` branch above accepts, so `link-account` returns:\n./docs/servers/elicitation.md:182:- Form mode carries a `message` and a flat JSON-Schema `requestedSchema`; the SDK validates accepted content against it.\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:4:description: Migrate MCP TypeScript SDK code from v1 (@modelcontextprotocol/sdk) to v2 (@modelcontextprotocol/core, /client, /server). Use when a user asks to migrate, upgrade, or port their MCP TypeScript code from v1 to v2.\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:50:    - [HTTP & headers](#http--headers)\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:122:  and `extra.requestInfo?.headers[…]` is remapped to `ctx.http?.req?.headers[…]`, but\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:124:  `requestInit.headers` need no rewrite — plain objects remain valid.)\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:125:  → [HTTP & headers](#http--headers)\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:148:  compilation, output-schema validation rules. → [Behavioral changes](#behavioral-changes)\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:524:the custom form, which validates against **your** schema (the spec schema is not\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:622:`CreateMessageResult | CreateMessageResultWithTools`, and validation selects the\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:769:your schema library's validation directly (e.g. Zod's `.safeParseAsync()`);\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:773:internal `standardSchemaToJsonSchema` / `validateStandardSchema` helpers are **not** part\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:782:### HTTP & headers\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:786:`ctx.http?.req?.headers` takes `.get()` instead of bracket access.\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:791:    requestInit: { headers: { Authorization: 'Bearer token' } }\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:793:const sessionId = extra.requestInfo?.headers['mcp-session-id'];\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:795:// v2 — requestInit is unchanged; only the header *read* changes\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:797:    requestInit: { headers: { Authorization: 'Bearer token' } }\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:799:const sessionId = ctx.http?.req?.headers.get('mcp-session-id');\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:804:`SSEClientTransport` options is a standard fetch `RequestInit`, so `headers` accepts\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:809:`StreamableHTTPClientTransport` now **appends** any custom `requestInit.headers.Accept`\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:817:(`validateHostHeader`, `localhostAllowedHostnames`, `hostHeaderValidationResponse`) are\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:820:Server entries validate the request `Content-Type` by its **parsed media type**, not a\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:825:hand-rolled clients that omit the header (or send a non-JSON type) must now set\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:828:clients always sent the correct header and are unaffected. Custom entries that\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:830:apply the same validation themselves — use the exported `isJsonContentType(header)`.\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:955:- Requests that require a session but omit the `Mcp-Session-Id` header still\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:956:  respond `400` with JSON-RPC `-32000` (`Bad Request: Mcp-Session-Id header is\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:994:| `InvalidResult`                       | Response result failed local schema validation                             |\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1115:- **Scoped credential invalidation on `invalid_client` / `unauthorized_client`.** The\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1117:  `invalidateCredentials('client')` then `invalidateCredentials('tokens')` — instead of\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1118:  v1's single `invalidateCredentials('all')`, deliberately preserving the stored\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1120:  A provider whose `invalidateCredentials()` implementation special-cases the `'all'`\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1167:alongside `authorizationCode` for RFC 9207 validation), `skipIssuerMetadataValidation?:\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1174:`transport.finishAuth()` and `auth()` now validate `iss` from the authorization callback\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1175:against the issuer recorded from validated AS metadata. A mismatched `iss` throws\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1181:The SDK does **not** validate `state`; compare it yourself before calling `finishAuth`:\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1190:`error_description` / `error_uri` from a callback that failed `iss` validation — those\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1338:const result = specTypeSchemas.CallToolResult['~standard'].validate(value);\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1342:every named type in the MCP spec — so you get autocomplete and a compile error on typos.\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1343:`specTypeSchemas.X` is a `StandardSchemaV1Sync<In, Out>` (`validate()` is synchronous).\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1344:`validate()` returns `{ value }` or `{ issues }` and never throws — unlike `.parse()` on\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1470:| relying on default `Ajv` being draft-07                            | `new AjvJsonSchemaValidator(new Ajv({ strict: false, validateFormats: true, validateSchema: false, allErrors: true }))` (import `Ajv`, `addFormats`, `AjvJsonSchemaValidator` from `…/validators/ajv`) |\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1505:- **Also unchanged: elicitation response validation.** `elicitInput`'s local validation\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1509:  tests pinning the local-validation message and custom validator wiring need no\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1515:- **The `MCP error <code>: ` message prefix is gone.** v1 prefixed relayed JSON-RPC\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1516:  error messages (`MCP error -32602: …`); v2's `ProtocolError.message` carries the\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1542:  first (header-mirroring scan, response-cache freshness, output-validator resolution),\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1574:  JSON-RPC schema validation still throw.\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1619:#### Server (deprecated accessors and app-factory Origin validation)\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1624:  localhost-class `host` now also validate the `Origin` header by default. Browser-served\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1626:  localhost allowlist; validation cannot be disabled for localhost binds). Requests\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1627:  without an `Origin` header are unaffected; a present `Origin` that cannot be parsed\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1631:  (`validateOriginHeader`, `localhostAllowedOrigins`, `originValidationResponse`) are in\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1659:- **`registerResource` reserves the `cacheHint` config key.** It is validated\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1680:- **`ElicitResult.content` values are typed and validated as\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1683:  objects now fails to compile (and fails schema validation) — narrow to the primitives\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1686:  used to delete `params._meta` before validation; it now passes `_meta` through (minus\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1689:- **`specTypeSchemas` validate the neutral model.** Result entries no longer accept\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1695:- **Inbound frames that fail message-shape validation are not answered.** v2 routes\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1758:`Mcp-Param-*` headers, and per-era wire codecs are covered in\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1801:  `setSupportedProtocolVersions` on the interface, `requestSignal` / `headers` /\n./docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md:1803:  per-request-stream cancellation and `Mcp-Param-*` header attachment\n./docs/advanced/gateway.md:6:A **gateway** — a proxy, a worker pool, any process that fronts one MCP server with many short-lived clients — probes the server once and reuses the answer for every connection after it.\n./docs/advanced/gateway.md:85:`request_count` is a tool on this page's example server that returns how many MCP requests reached the process. Five clients are connected by now — `bootstrap`, `worker`, three replicas — and the server has answered two requests:\n./test/e2e/fixtures/stdio-server.ts:2: * Runnable stdio MCP server fixture for the transport:stdio:* e2e tests.\n./docs/migration/index.md:8:# MCP TypeScript SDK — Migration Guides\n./docs/advanced/wire-schemas.md:6:`@modelcontextprotocol/core` exports the **wire schemas** — the exact Zod constants the SDK validates protocol and OAuth payloads against — for code that holds raw JSON instead of SDK objects.\n./docs/advanced/wire-schemas.md:10:`CallToolResultSchema.safeParse` validates an upstream body before you relay it.\n./docs/advanced/wire-schemas.md:12:```ts source=\"../../examples/guides/advanced/wire-schemas.examples.ts#wireSchemas_validateResult\"\n./docs/advanced/wire-schemas.md:33:```ts source=\"../../examples/guides/advanced/wire-schemas.examples.ts#wireSchemas_validateResult_invalid\"\n./docs/advanced/wire-schemas.md:57:If you build with `McpServer` or `Client`, skip this package: [tools](../servers/tools.md) arrive in your handler already validated, and [tool calls](../clients/calling.md) come back as typed results. Reach for `@modelcontextprotocol/core` when nothing stands between you and the JSON — gateways, proxies, test harnesses, [worker fleets](./gateway.md).\n./docs/advanced/wire-schemas.md:63:Every named type in the spec has a matching constant, `<SpecType>Schema`. When you do not yet know which one you hold, `JSONRPCMessageSchema` validates the undecoded envelope.\n./docs/advanced/wire-schemas.md:78:Parse the envelope once, branch on `method`, then validate with the per-method request schema before forwarding.\n./docs/advanced/wire-schemas.md:102:For everything beyond validation — sessions, capability negotiation, request correlation — build on the SDK instead: see the [low-level server](./low-level-server.md).\n./docs/advanced/wire-schemas.md:106:The second export group covers OAuth and OpenID discovery. `OAuthMetadataSchema` validates an authorization server's metadata document.\n./docs/advanced/wire-schemas.md:156:- Every spec type has a `<Name>Schema` constant; `JSONRPCMessageSchema` validates the undecoded envelope.\n./docs/servers/logging-progress-cancellation.md:77:Log to `stderr` (stdio servers) or use OpenTelemetry instead. **MCP logging** is deprecated as of protocol version 2026-07-28 (SEP-2577) and stays functional through the deprecation window (at least twelve months) — see the [deprecated features registry](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/draft/deprecated).\n./docs/servers/logging-progress-cancellation.md:90:    'validate-records',\n./docs/servers/logging-progress-cancellation.md:114:Calling `validate-records` with one bad record delivers both log notifications before the result:\n./docs/servers/logging-progress-cancellation.md:204:- `ctx.mcpReq.log(level, data)` sends `notifications/message` once the `logging` capability is declared; MCP logging is deprecated (SEP-2577).\n./docs/migration/support-2026-07-28.md:32:- [`Mcp-Param-*` and standard headers (SEP-2243)](#mcp-param--and-standard-headers-sep-2243)\n./docs/migration/support-2026-07-28.md:83:deployed 2025 servers commonly have CORS allow-lists that predate the 2026 headers.\n./docs/migration/support-2026-07-28.md:302:The 2026-07-28 specification's authorization requirements (RFC 9207 `iss` validation,\n./docs/migration/support-2026-07-28.md:322:no longer switches the era per message — it is validated against the instance era, and a\n./docs/migration/support-2026-07-28.md:339:| relying on `isCallToolResult` to reject wire-only members                                    | guards validate neutral shapes (loose passthrough); validate raw wire traffic with a transport-level parse |\n./docs/migration/support-2026-07-28.md:396:  before validation. On a 2026-era exchange `resultType` is REQUIRED; an absent value is\n./docs/migration/support-2026-07-28.md:408:for transports that classify inbound messages at the edge; dispatch validates it against\n./docs/migration/support-2026-07-28.md:451:structural read with an unvalidated cast), two typed readers ship from\n./docs/migration/support-2026-07-28.md:455:  Standard Schema, e.g. a zod object): validates the untrusted accepted content and\n./docs/migration/support-2026-07-28.md:492:- Responses arrive as the bare result objects, era-wire-shape-validated only:\n./docs/migration/support-2026-07-28.md:494:  exactly as on the modern era — so the handler validates with the\n./docs/migration/support-2026-07-28.md:575:## `Mcp-Param-*` and standard headers (SEP-2243)\n./docs/migration/support-2026-07-28.md:578:arguments designated with `x-mcp-header` in the tool's `inputSchema` into\n./docs/migration/support-2026-07-28.md:579:`Mcp-Param-{Name}` HTTP request headers (Base64-sentinel-encoded where needed), and\n./docs/migration/support-2026-07-28.md:580:`createMcpHandler` rejects a `tools/call` whose `Mcp-Param-*` headers are missing for a\n./docs/migration/support-2026-07-28.md:583:`Mcp-Name` standard header on every modern-enveloped request, and `createMcpHandler`\n./docs/migration/support-2026-07-28.md:584:validates the SEP-2243 standard headers (`MCP-Protocol-Version`, `Mcp-Method`,\n./docs/migration/support-2026-07-28.md:594:directly so mirroring and output-schema validation run without a prior `tools/list`),\n./docs/migration/support-2026-07-28.md:595:`TransportSendOptions.headers` (per-request HTTP headers; reserved standard/auth header\n./docs/migration/support-2026-07-28.md:596:names are skipped). Browser clients skip mirroring (dynamically named headers cannot be\n./docs/advanced/custom-methods.md:7:A **custom method** is a JSON-RPC method outside the MCP specification. Prefix it with a vendor namespace — `acme/search`, never a bare `search` — so it can never collide with a spec method.\n./docs/advanced/custom-methods.md:27:The SDK validates incoming `params` against `SearchParams` before the handler runs; `result` types the handler's return value. A spec method never takes the schema bundle — `setRequestHandler('tools/call', handler)` resolves its schemas from the method name.\n./docs/advanced/custom-methods.md:40:Every call on this page comes from an in-memory `Client` connected to the server above — [Test a server](../testing.md) shows that wiring. For a non-spec method, `client.request` takes the request and a result schema; the SDK validates the response against it before the promise resolves and infers the return type from it.\n./docs/advanced/custom-methods.md:47:The handler's return value comes back validated and typed:\n./docs/advanced/custom-methods.md:127:- The SDK validates incoming `params` before the handler runs and rejects what fails with an `Invalid params` error; `result` types the handler's return value.\n./docs/advanced/custom-methods.md:128:- `client.request(request, ResultSchema)` is the calling side; the SDK validates the response against the schema.\n./packages/core-internal/src/validators/ajvProvider.examples.ts:36:    const ajv = new Ajv2020({ strict: false, validateSchema: false, allErrors: true });\n./packages/core-internal/src/validators/ajvProvider.examples.ts:53:    const ajv = new Ajv2020({ strict: false, validateSchema: false, allErrors: true });\n./packages/codemod/src/cli.ts:67:program.name('mcp-codemod').description('Codemod to migrate MCP TypeScript SDK code between versions').version(version);\n./packages/codemod/src/cli.ts:137:                        console.log(`No MCP SDK imports found under ${resolvedDir} — nothing to migrate.\\n`);\n./test/e2e/fixtures/dual-era-stdio-server.ts:2: * Runnable dual-era stdio MCP server fixture for the dual-era stdio e2e cells.\n./docs/advanced/custom-transports.md:81:The whole MCP handshake and the tool call run over the loopback; the handler's `content` comes back unchanged:\n./docs/advanced/custom-transports.md:162:`setSupportedProtocolVersions` is the third optional member: `connect()` passes the local side's accepted versions into it, which is how the HTTP server transports know what to allow in the `MCP-Protocol-Version` header.\n./docs/advanced/custom-transports.md:175:        headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json', ...options?.headers },\n./packages/core-internal/src/validators/types.ts:17: * Result of a JSON Schema validation operation\n./packages/core-internal/src/validators/types.ts:24: * A validator function that validates data against a JSON Schema\n./packages/core-internal/src/validators/types.ts:36: * - Provide clear error messages on validation failure\n./docs/advanced/schema-libraries.md:28:From that one schema the SDK derives the JSON Schema the model sees, validates arguments before your handler runs, and infers the handler's argument types — `name` is `string`, `times` is `number | undefined`.\n./docs/advanced/schema-libraries.md:37:      text: 'Input validation error: Invalid arguments for tool greet: times: times must be at most 5 (was 99)'\n./docs/advanced/schema-libraries.md:117:The SDK validates `structuredContent` against the ArkType schema before the result leaves your server. Calling `measure` with `{ name: 'Ada' }` returns both renderings:\n./docs/advanced/schema-libraries.md:156:`edge` runs the same two validation paths through `@cfworker/json-schema` instead of AJV, on any runtime.\n./packages/core-internal/src/validators/cfWorkerProvider.ts:4: * This provider uses @cfworker/json-schema for validation without code generation,\n./packages/core-internal/src/validators/cfWorkerProvider.ts:33: * Default validates as **JSON Schema 2020-12** (SEP-1613). Schemas declaring a different\n./packages/core-internal/src/validators/cfWorkerProvider.ts:59:     * @param options.shortcircuit - If `true`, stop validation after first error (default: `true`)\n./packages/core-internal/src/validators/cfWorkerProvider.ts:61:     * `$schema` check is skipped. When omitted, the provider validates as 2020-12 and rejects\n./packages/core-internal/src/validators/cfWorkerProvider.ts:75:     * @returns A validator function that validates input data\n./packages/core-internal/src/validators/cfWorkerProvider.ts:89:                    `{ draft } to CfWorkerJsonSchemaValidator to validate other dialects.`\n./packages/core-internal/src/validators/cfWorkerProvider.ts:98:            const result = validator.validate(input);\n./packages/core-internal/src/validators/ajvProvider.ts:14: * than letting the engine crash on an opaque internal error or silently mis-validate.\n./packages/core-internal/src/validators/ajvProvider.ts:41:        validateFormats: true,\n./packages/core-internal/src/validators/ajvProvider.ts:42:        validateSchema: false,\n./packages/core-internal/src/validators/ajvProvider.ts:53: * Default validates as **JSON Schema 2020-12** (SEP-1613). Schemas declaring a different\n./packages/core-internal/src/validators/ajvProvider.ts:54: * `$schema` are rejected with a plain `Error`; pass a pre-configured Ajv instance to validate\n./packages/core-internal/src/validators/ajvProvider.ts:69: * const ajv = new Ajv2020({ strict: false, validateSchema: false, allErrors: true });\n./packages/core-internal/src/validators/ajvProvider.ts:76: * const ajv = new Ajv2020({ strict: false, validateSchema: false, allErrors: true });\n./packages/core-internal/src/validators/ajvProvider.ts:90:     * `strict: false`, `validateFormats: true`, `validateSchema: false`, `allErrors: true`, and\n./packages/core-internal/src/validators/ajvProvider.ts:112:                    `Ajv instance to AjvJsonSchemaValidator(ajv) to validate other dialects.`\n./packages/core-internal/src/validators/ajvProvider.ts:144: * const ajv = new Ajv({ strict: false, validateFormats: true, validateSchema: false, allErrors: true });\n./packages/core-internal/src/validators/ajvProvider.ts:149: * (omitting `validateSchema: false` makes a 2020-12-stamped `$schema` fail with an opaque\n./packages/core-internal/src/validators/ajvProvider.ts:151: * validation that the v1 default had).\n./docs/clients/calling.md:74:When an earlier `listTools()` gave the client the tool's `outputSchema`, `callTool` validates `structuredContent` against it and rejects a result that does not match.\n./packages/core-internal/CHANGELOG.md:14:    - `resultType` is no longer modeled by any neutral wire schema: `EmptyResultSchema` (strict) now rejects `{resultType}` bodies; on 2025-era connections a foreign `resultType` is stripped before validation instead of rejected; the member exists only inside the 2026-era codec, which requires it.\n./packages/core-internal/CHANGELOG.md:16:    - Custom (3-arg) handlers now receive `_meta` minus the reserved envelope keys instead of having it deleted before params validation.\n./packages/core-internal/CHANGELOG.md:30:  `mode: { pin: '<version>' }` negotiates exactly the pinned modern revision with no fallback. Probe policy lives under `probe: { timeoutMs? }` — the probe inherits the standard request timeout. The probe's `MCP-Protocol-Version`/`Mcp-Method` headers derive from the probe\n./packages/core-internal/CHANGELOG.md:31:  message body; the transport version slot is never touched during negotiation, so legacy-era traffic carries zero 2026 headers by construction. Adds the `SdkErrorCode.EraNegotiationFailed` code for negotiation-phase connect failures.\n./packages/core-internal/CHANGELOG.md:35:- [#2286](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/pull/2286) [`1823aae`](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/commit/1823aae891837ebb5e3db885ec635f9efefd5771) Thanks [@felixweinberger](https://github.com/felixweinberger)! - Implement RFC 9207 / RFC 8414 §3.3 OAuth issuer validation (SEP-2468). `discoverAuthorizationServerMetadata()` now rejects metadata whose `issuer` does not match the discovery URL (opt out via `skipIssuerValidation` / `AuthOptions.skipIssuerMetadataValidation` — security-weakening). `auth()`, `exchangeAuthorization()`, `fetchToken()`, and `transport.finishAuth(code, iss?)` now validate the authorization-callback `iss` against the recorded issuer before redeeming the code; new `IssuerMismatchError` and `validateAuthorizationResponseIssuer()` are exported.\n./packages/core-internal/CHANGELOG.md:39:- [#2286](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/pull/2286) [`1823aae`](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/commit/1823aae891837ebb5e3db885ec635f9efefd5771) Thanks [@felixweinberger](https://github.com/felixweinberger)! - Results of the cacheable 2026-07-28 operations (`tools/list`, `prompts/list`, `resources/list`, `resources/templates/list`, `resources/read`, `server/discover`) now always carry the revision's required `ttlMs`/`cacheScope` fields when served on that revision, defaulting to `ttlMs: 0` / `cacheScope: 'private'`. Servers can configure the emitted values with the new `ServerOptions.cacheHints` option (per operation) and the new `cacheHint` member of the `registerResource` config (per resource); resolution is per field, most specific author first: cache fields returned by a handler win over the per-resource hint, which wins over the per-operation hint, and configured hints are validated at construction/registration time (`RangeError` on invalid values). Responses on 2025-era connections are unchanged and never carry these fields. Note for untyped callers: `registerResource` now interprets a `cacheHint` key in its config object — it is validated and kept out of the resource's list metadata, where it was previously passed through as ordinary metadata.\n./packages/core-internal/CHANGELOG.md:43:- [#2286](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/pull/2286) [`1823aae`](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/commit/1823aae891837ebb5e3db885ec635f9efefd5771) Thanks [@felixweinberger](https://github.com/felixweinberger)! - Add `SdkErrorCode.MethodNotSupportedByProtocolVersion`: a typed local error raised before anything reaches the transport when a spec method is sent toward a peer whose negotiated protocol version's wire era does not define it (for example `tasks/get` toward a 2026-07-28 peer). The protocol layer now resolves a per-era wire codec from the connection's negotiated protocol version (instance state on `Client`/`Server`, with the legacy era as the pre-negotiation default) and resolves per-method schemas at dispatch time instead of registration time; an edge classification on an inbound message is validated against that instance era, and a mismatch is rejected as an entry/routing error. Behavior on existing (2025-era) connections is unchanged.\n./packages/core-internal/CHANGELOG.md:52:- [#2381](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/pull/2381) [`f0bf785`](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/commit/f0bf785239af67f0cfce24575fec9c1193deaff1) Thanks [@felixweinberger](https://github.com/felixweinberger)! - Serve `input_required` handlers on 2025-era connections: the legacy shim (on by default) converts each embedded request of an `input_required` return into a real server→client request (`elicitation/create`, `sampling/createMessage`, `roots/list`) over the live session — stamped with the originating request's id for stream association — and re-enters the handler with the collected `inputResponses` until a final result. Handlers are written once in the 2026 `inputRequired(...)` style and serve both eras; the previous loud `-32603` failure remains available via `ServerOptions.inputRequired.legacyShim: false`. Knobs: `inputRequired.maxRounds` (default 8) and `inputRequired.roundTimeoutMs` (default 600 000 ms per leg; legs carry a progressToken so a client reporting progress mid-leg resets the leg timeout). Semantics mirror the modern client driver exactly: per-round replaced `inputResponses`, byte-exact `requestState` echo with the verify hook running every round, paced requestState-only rounds, and elicitation accepted content passed through UNVALIDATED (the handler validates via the schema-aware `acceptedContent` overload, exactly as on the 2026 era). URL-mode legs synthesize the `elicitationId` the 2025-11-25 wire requires. Failures surface per family (`tools/call` → `isError` tool result; `prompts/get` / `resources/read` → JSON-RPC error); stateless legacy HTTP degrades to a clean capability refusal; the shim emits no progress of its own (the originating progressToken is the handler's single must-increase stream — the shim never adds a second author to it).\n./packages/core-internal/CHANGELOG.md:56:    New typed readers for `inputResponses`, exported from `@modelcontextprotocol/server`: a schema-aware `acceptedContent(responses, key, schema)` overload (validates untrusted accepted content against any synchronous Standard Schema) and `inputResponse(responses, key)` (discriminated `missing | elicit | sampling | roots` view, for decline/cancel detection and the non-elicitation kinds). Content conveniences like text extraction stay in application code as one-liners over the discriminated view.\n./packages/core-internal/CHANGELOG.md:69:  to request additional client input in-band; the structured-content requirement and the tools/call result-schema validation are skipped for that return, the encode seam emits it as `resultType: 'input_required'`, and the handler reads the responses on re-entry from\n./packages/core-internal/CHANGELOG.md:95:- [#2286](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/pull/2286) [`1823aae`](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/commit/1823aae891837ebb5e3db885ec635f9efefd5771) Thanks [@felixweinberger](https://github.com/felixweinberger)! - SEP-1613 / SEP-2106 (JSON Schema 2020-12 posture): the Node default JSON Schema validator is now `Ajv2020` (true draft 2020-12) instead of the draft-07 `Ajv` class — `$defs`/`prefixItems`/`unevaluatedProperties`/`dependentRequired` are now enforced where they were previously silently ignored; to opt back, construct the draft-07 instance with the v1 defaults — `const ajv = new Ajv({ strict: false, validateFormats: true, validateSchema: false, allErrors: true }); addFormats(ajv);` — and pass `new AjvJsonSchemaValidator(ajv)`. Schemas declaring a `$schema` other than 2020-12 are rejected with a clear error rather than mis-validating. `outputSchema` may now have a non-object root and `CallToolResult.structuredContent` is widened to `unknown` (a deliberate source-level break for typed consumers — see the migration guide for the narrowing pattern). Toward 2025-era clients McpServer wraps a non-object `outputSchema` (and the matching `structuredContent`) in a `{result: …}` envelope so the tool stays callable, with same-document `$ref`/`$dynamicRef` pointers rewritten to keep resolving — low-level `Server` users (those bypassing `McpServer` and registering a `tools/call` handler directly) get the same wrap by routing the result through the new `Server.projectCallToolResult(result, advertisedOutputSchema)`. Independently, on every era (the SEP's MUST applies regardless of client version), McpServer auto-appends a `TextContent` JSON serialisation when a handler returns non-object `structuredContent` without its own text block. The `structuredContent` presence check is `!== undefined` (not falsy) on both sides. Thanks @mattzcarey (#2249).\n./packages/core-internal/CHANGELOG.md:97:- [#2286](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/pull/2286) [`1823aae`](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/commit/1823aae891837ebb5e3db885ec635f9efefd5771) Thanks [@felixweinberger](https://github.com/felixweinberger)! - SEP-2243 `Mcp-Param-*` client-side mirroring (protocol revision 2026-07-28). On a 2026-07-28 connection over Streamable HTTP, `Client.callTool()` now mirrors tool arguments designated with `x-mcp-header` in the tool's `inputSchema` into `Mcp-Param-{Name}` HTTP headers (with the spec's `=?base64?…?=` sentinel encoding for values that are not safe plain-ASCII field values), and on a non-stdio modern connection `Client.listTools()` (and the client's internal `tools/list` cache) exclude tool definitions whose `x-mcp-header` declarations violate the spec's constraints, logging a warning naming the tool and the reason. The legacy-era `callTool` and `listTools` paths are unchanged at the wire level. Browser environments skip mirroring (dynamically named headers cannot be statically allow-listed for credentialed CORS); a conforming SEP-2243 server will reject a `tools/call` whose body carries a non-null value for an `x-mcp-header` parameter when the matching header is absent, so calling such a tool with that argument from a browser is a known limitation. New `CallToolRequestOptions.toolDefinition` lets callers supply the tool definition directly so mirroring and output-schema validation can run without a prior `tools/list`. `TransportSendOptions.headers` is added (additive, optional) for per-request HTTP headers; the Streamable HTTP transport skips reserved standard/auth header names (`authorization`, `mcp-protocol-version`, `mcp-method`, `mcp-name`, `mcp-session-id`, `content-type`); transports that share a single channel (stdio, in-memory) ignore it.\n./packages/core-internal/CHANGELOG.md:99:    The Streamable HTTP transport now emits the `Mcp-Name` standard header on every modern-enveloped request (`params.name` for `tools/call`/`prompts/get`, `params.uri` for `resources/read`), sentinel-encoded.\n./packages/core-internal/CHANGELOG.md:103:- [#2286](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/pull/2286) [`1823aae`](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/commit/1823aae891837ebb5e3db885ec635f9efefd5771) Thanks [@felixweinberger](https://github.com/felixweinberger)! - SEP-2243 standard-header server-side validation (protocol revision 2026-07-28). On the modern (2026-07-28) serving path, `createMcpHandler` now enforces the required `Mcp-Method` and `Mcp-Name` standard request headers in addition to the existing `MCP-Protocol-Version` and `Mcp-Method` cross-checks: a modern request without an `Mcp-Method` header, a `tools/call` / `prompts/get` / `resources/read` request without an `Mcp-Name` header, an `Mcp-Name` header carrying an invalid `=?base64?…?=` sentinel, and an `Mcp-Name` header whose (decoded) value disagrees with the body's `params.name` / `params.uri` are all rejected with `400 Bad Request` and JSON-RPC `-32020` (`HeaderMismatch`). The 2025-era serving paths are unchanged.\n./packages/core-internal/CHANGELOG.md:106:    - `@modelcontextprotocol/server`: the `mcpNameHeader` field on `InboundHttpRequest`, and the `'standard-header-validation'` member of `InboundValidationRung` (with `client-capabilities` / `param-header-validation` renumbered).\n./packages/core-internal/CHANGELOG.md:134:- [#2286](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/pull/2286) [`1823aae`](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/commit/1823aae891837ebb5e3db885ec635f9efefd5771) Thanks [@felixweinberger](https://github.com/felixweinberger)! - Pin the modern (2026-07-28) HTTP serving path's rejection codes to the assignments the published conformance suite asserts: a header/body cross-check mismatch (`MCP-Protocol-Version` or `Mcp-Method` disagreeing with the request body) is now rejected with `-32020` (HeaderMismatch), and a request whose protocol-version header names a modern revision but whose body is missing the `_meta` envelope (or its required protocol-version key) is rejected with `-32602` invalid params naming the missing key(s). Both keep HTTP 400. These cells previously emitted a provisional `-32004` while the upstream error-code discussion was open. The envelope-less rejection on a modern-only endpoint (`-32022` with the supported-versions list), the 2025-era serving paths, and the client-side probe handling are unchanged.\n./packages/core-internal/CHANGELOG.md:143:    - The error code `-32001` emitted for HTTP header/body mismatches is now defined by the draft schema (`HEADER_MISMATCH`); the emitted behavior is unchanged (documentation only).\n./packages/core-internal/CHANGELOG.md:171:    Response result-schema validation failure now rejects with `SdkError(InvalidResult)` instead of a raw `ZodError`. Adds `SdkErrorCode.InvalidResult`.\n./packages/core-internal/CHANGELOG.md:191:    The `jsonSchemaValidator` interface remains the public extension point for replacing validation entirely with a custom implementation.\n./packages/core-internal/CHANGELOG.md:239:  unknown tools and resources per MCP spec.\n./packages/core-internal/CHANGELOG.md:251:    `StandardSchemaWithJSON` (requires both `~standard.validate` and `~standard.jsonSchema`) instead of the Zod-specific `AnySchema` type.\n./packages/core-internal/CHANGELOG.md:283:    - Removed unused exports from `@modelcontextprotocol/core-internal`: `SchemaInput`, `schemaToJson`, `parseSchemaAsync`, `getSchemaShape`, `getSchemaDescription`, `isOptionalSchema`, `unwrapOptionalSchema`. Use the new `standardSchemaToJsonSchema` and `validateStandardSchema`\n./packages/core-internal/CHANGELOG.md:293:  `ResourceSchema` to match the MCP specification\n./packages/core-internal/CHANGELOG.md:324:  `standardSchemaToJsonSchema` emits `type: \"object\"` at the root, fixing discriminated-union tool/prompt schemas that previously produced `{oneOf: [...]}` without the MCP-required top-level type. Also throws a clear error when given an explicitly non-object schema (e.g.\n./packages/core-internal/CHANGELOG.md:332:  schema validation still throw.\n./docs/clients/connect.md:10:`Client` takes a name and a version; `StreamableHTTPClientTransport` takes the server's MCP endpoint URL.\n./docs/clients/middleware.md:16:    const headers = new Headers(init?.headers);\n./docs/clients/middleware.md:17:    headers.set('X-Request-Source', 'reports-cli');\n./docs/clients/middleware.md:18:    return next(input, { ...init, headers });\n./docs/clients/middleware.md:26:Every request this transport sends now carries the header — including the requests the SDK sends that you never wrote, like `initialize`.\n./docs/clients/middleware.md:79:The `POST`s are `initialize`, the `notifications/initialized` notification, and your `tools/call`; the `GET` opens the server-to-client stream, which this server declines. Pass `statusLevel: 400` to log only failures, `includeRequestHeaders` / `includeResponseHeaders` to add headers to each line, and `logger` to replace the formatter entirely.\n./docs/clients/middleware.md:82:The default logger writes to `console.log` and `console.error`. In a process whose stdout carries an MCP stdio transport, pass your own `logger` so these lines stay off that stream.\n./docs/clients/middleware.md:87:`withOAuth(provider, serverUrl)` is the OAuth flow expressed as one middleware layer: it adds the `Authorization` header, and on a `401` it re-authenticates against `serverUrl` and retries the request once.\n./packages/core-internal/src/validators/types.examples.ts:12:// Stub for hypothetical schema validation function\n./packages/middleware/fastify/src/fastify.ts:8: * Options for creating an MCP Fastify application.\n./packages/middleware/fastify/src/fastify.ts:19:     * If provided, host header validation will be applied using this list.\n./packages/middleware/fastify/src/fastify.ts:28:     * List of allowed origin hostnames for Origin header validation.\n./packages/middleware/fastify/src/fastify.ts:29:     * If provided, Origin validation will be applied using this list (port-agnostic,\n./packages/middleware/fastify/src/fastify.ts:32:     * When omitted, Origin validation is automatically enabled for localhost-class\n./packages/middleware/fastify/src/fastify.ts:33:     * binds (the same condition as host validation): requests without an `Origin`\n./packages/middleware/fastify/src/fastify.ts:34:     * header pass, while a present `Origin` whose hostname is not localhost-class\n./packages/middleware/fastify/src/fastify.ts:41: * Creates a Fastify application pre-configured for MCP servers.\n./packages/middleware/fastify/src/fastify.ts:48: * for MCP Streamable HTTP endpoints.\n./packages/middleware/fastify/src/fastify.ts:76:    // If allowedHosts is explicitly provided, use that for validation\n./packages/middleware/fastify/src/fastify.ts:94:    // Origin validation follows the same arming ladder as host validation:\n./packages/middleware/fastify/src/fastify.ts:96:    // by default. Requests without an Origin header always pass.\n./packages/core-internal/src/validators/fromJsonSchema.ts:37:            validate: (data: unknown): StandardSchemaV1.Result<T> => {\n./docs/clients/oauth.md:51:        client_name: 'My MCP Client',\n./docs/clients/oauth.md:108:// The SDK does not validate `state` — compare it to the value your provider generated.\n./docs/clients/oauth.md:118:`finishAuth(params)` extracts `code`, validates the RFC 9207 `iss` parameter, exchanges the code at the authorization server discovery resolved before the redirect, and saves the tokens through your provider. The second `connect()` finds those tokens and completes without another redirect.\n./docs/clients/oauth.md:148:The SDK binds tokens to your server with the RFC 8707 `resource` parameter: when the server publishes protected resource metadata (RFC 9728), the SDK checks the metadata's `resource` against the server URL and attaches it to the authorization redirect and every token request. Override `validateResourceURL` to force the value — return the URL to send, or `undefined` to omit the parameter.\n./docs/clients/oauth.md:150:```ts source=\"../../examples/guides/clients/oauth.examples.ts#validateResourceURL_pin\"\n./docs/clients/oauth.md:152:    async validateResourceURL(serverUrl: string | URL, resource?: string): Promise<URL | undefined> {\n./docs/clients/oauth.md:168:- `finishAuth(params)` with the whole callback query validates `iss` (RFC 9207) and exchanges the code.\n./docs/clients/oauth.md:171:- `validateResourceURL` overrides the RFC 8707 `resource` parameter the SDK sends.\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/auth.ts:4: * Reusable URL validation that disallows `javascript:` scheme\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/auth.ts:190:         * OIDC Dynamic Client Registration `application_type`. MCP clients MUST set\n./packages/codemod/CHANGELOG.md:20:- [#2402](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/pull/2402) [`a400259`](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/commit/a4002596b914c675d17ac22471d1287976dbb52a) Thanks [@felixweinberger](https://github.com/felixweinberger)! - First beta release of SDK v2 with support for the MCP 2026-07-28 specification\n./packages/codemod/CHANGELOG.md:31:- [#2393](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/pull/2393) [`e4e8b22`](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/commit/e4e8b22dff6c48b9a46fd960c9171aed44a996a8) Thanks [@felixweinberger](https://github.com/felixweinberger)! - Migration-sweep batch. The ErrorCode split now coordinates with the surrounding check: an all-SDK condition's `instanceof ProtocolError`/`McpError` guard is rewritten to `SdkError`, a guard covering both enums gets a marker asking for a split, and an `ErrorCode` import with no rewritable member access on a v2 specifier is dropped with a marker instead of failing at module link time. Wrapping a raw shape with `z.object()` adds `import { z } from 'zod'` when the file has no `z` value binding (a non-import `z` binding gets a marker instead). The context-parameter rewrite finds the trailing `extra` parameter, covering the three-argument `registerResource` template callback without flagging its `variables` argument. Resource-server auth helpers routed to the frozen server-legacy copy get a marker on value imports and barrel re-exports (an info note for type-only imports), every rewritten `SdkHttpError` constructor site gets a marker, and single-argument `finishAuth(...)` calls in files the run changes get a run-log note (the one-argument `URLSearchParams` form is valid v2, so the note never re-fires on already-migrated trees). The codemod accepts a single source file as target — source rewrites scope to that file and manifest changes are reported, not applied — and the no-changes summary distinguishes \"already on the v2 packages\", \"still on the v1 SDK under a transform subset\", and \"no MCP SDK imports found\".\n./packages/codemod/CHANGELOG.md:39:- [#2393](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/pull/2393) [`e4e8b22`](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/commit/e4e8b22dff6c48b9a46fd960c9171aed44a996a8) Thanks [@felixweinberger](https://github.com/felixweinberger)! - Fixes from migrating two large consumers. Registrations nested inside another handler's body no longer crash the transform with a whole-file rollback (calls process inner-first). Legacy `.tool()`/`.prompt()`/`.resource()` calls migrate without a direct `McpServer` import when their shape matches the v1 signature AND the receiver is named like an MCP server (`server`, `harness.mcp`, `this.mockServer`); other receivers are left alone, without hard markers, since their type is unknown to the codemod. `setRequestHandler`/`setNotificationHandler` with a schema _expression_ first argument get a marker pointing at the typed two-argument or custom three-argument form instead of being skipped silently, and `removeRequestHandler`/`removeNotificationHandler` with `Schema.shape.method.value` arguments rewrite to the method string. Destructured trailing callback parameters only count as the context when their keys look like context members, so template-variable destructures stop collecting false markers. The manifest zod note only appears for manifests that actually take part in the migration.\n./packages/codemod/CHANGELOG.md:47:- [#2393](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/pull/2393) [`e4e8b22`](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/commit/e4e8b22dff6c48b9a46fd960c9171aed44a996a8) Thanks [@felixweinberger](https://github.com/felixweinberger)! - The explicit-`undefined` result-schema removal now requires the same proof as the schema-identifier path: `request()` calls must carry a provably literal spec method, and `callTool()` calls whose first argument is a primitive are left alone. Previously, any file importing an MCP package could have the middle argument deleted from an unrelated `.request()` / `.callTool()` member call on a non-SDK receiver (e.g. a bespoke `end.request('ping', undefined, id)` helper), corrupting the call.\n./packages/codemod/CHANGELOG.md:61:  `specTypeSchemas.X['~standard'].validate(...)`) is removed.\n./docs/clients/caching.md:55:`'bypass'` fetches without reading or writing: it leaves the cache byte-untouched, including the `tools/list` entry the SDK itself reads for output validation when you [call tools](./calling.md).\n./packages/middleware/fastify/src/middleware/originValidation.ts:1:import { localhostAllowedOrigins, validateOriginHeader } from '@modelcontextprotocol/server';\n./packages/middleware/fastify/src/middleware/originValidation.ts:5: * Fastify onRequest hook for Origin header validation.\n./packages/middleware/fastify/src/middleware/originValidation.ts:6: * Validates the `Origin` header hostname (port-agnostic) against an allowed list.\n./packages/middleware/fastify/src/middleware/originValidation.ts:8: * Browsers attach an `Origin` header to cross-origin requests; validating it —\n./packages/middleware/fastify/src/middleware/originValidation.ts:9: * alongside Host header validation — protects localhost and development servers\n./packages/middleware/fastify/src/middleware/originValidation.ts:11: * `Origin` header pass (non-browser MCP clients do not send one); a present\n./packages/middleware/fastify/src/middleware/originValidation.ts:25:        const result = validateOriginHeader(request.headers.origin, allowedOriginHostnames);\n./packages/middleware/fastify/src/middleware/originValidation.ts:40: * Convenience hook for localhost Origin validation.\n./docs/clients/machine-auth.md:43:The transport calls `token()` before every request and sets the `Authorization` header from whatever it returns. Without `onUnauthorized`, a 401 throws `UnauthorizedError`. Add `onUnauthorized(ctx)` to refresh the credential and the transport retries the request once.\n./docs/clients/machine-auth.md:63:**Cross-app access** (Enterprise Managed Authorization, SEP-990) lets a service reach an MCP server for a user who already authenticated with the enterprise IdP, with no second consent screen. Two exchanges get it there: the IdP ID Token becomes a **JWT Authorization Grant** (RFC 8693), and that grant becomes an MCP access token (RFC 7523).\n./docs/clients/machine-auth.md:89:The SDK discovers the MCP server's authorization server and resource URL (RFC 9728) before it calls `assertion`, then hands them in on `ctx` together with the negotiated `scope` and the transport's `fetchFn`. Pass them through so the IdP issues a grant bound to the right audience and resource.\n./docs/clients/machine-auth.md:97:- `exchangeJwtAuthGrant` exchanges a JWT Authorization Grant for an access token at the MCP server's authorization server (RFC 7523).\n./docs/clients/machine-auth.md:106:- `CrossAppAccessProvider` chains an enterprise IdP token through a JWT Authorization Grant to an MCP access token (SEP-990), and both exchanges are exported standalone.\n./test/e2e/scenarios/elicitation.test.ts:334:verifies('elicitation:form:response-validation', async ({ transport }: TestArgs) => {\n./packages/middleware/fastify/src/middleware/hostHeaderValidation.ts:1:import { localhostAllowedHostnames, validateHostHeader } from '@modelcontextprotocol/server';\n./packages/middleware/fastify/src/middleware/hostHeaderValidation.ts:6: * Validates `Host` header hostname (port-agnostic) against an allowed list.\n./packages/middleware/fastify/src/middleware/hostHeaderValidation.ts:24:        const result = validateHostHeader(request.headers.host, allowedHostnames);\n./packages/core-internal/src/wire/rev2026-07-28/encodeContract.ts:59: *   the SDK does not validate the string, so emitting a `resultType` the\n./packages/core-internal/src/wire/rev2026-07-28/encodeContract.ts:92: * defaults. Handler-returned values are validated at encode time (`ttlMs`\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:2: * Inbound HTTP request classification and the inbound validation ladder\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:18: *   a validation error, never a silent fall back to legacy handling).\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:20: * - The `MCP-Protocol-Version` header is a cross-check only: it never\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:22: *   between header and body is an explicit ladder outcome.\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:24: *   spec, so for notification POSTs without a body claim the modern header is\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:25: *   determinative; the `Mcp-Method` header is validated against the body when\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:29: *   malformed claim is — never silently resolved against the header.\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:30: *   The notification-POST header cross-checks here are an SDK-defensive\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:31: *   posture, not a spec requirement: the spec leaves header rules for posted\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:53: * - A header/body cross-check mismatch (the `MCP-Protocol-Version` header\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:54: *   disagreeing with the body, or the `Mcp-Method` header disagreeing with the\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:56: * - A request whose protocol-version header names a modern revision but whose\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:71:import { envelopeClaimVersion, hasEnvelopeClaim, requestMetaOf, validateEnvelopeMeta } from './envelope';\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:72:// Value encoding is shared between the standard `Mcp-Name` header and the\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:73:// custom `Mcp-Param-*` headers; the codec module already imports the\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:87: * extracts the two protocol headers; the classifier never touches a request\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:93:    /** The value of the `MCP-Protocol-Version` header, when present. */\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:95:    /** The value of the `Mcp-Method` header, when present. */\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:97:    /** The value of the `Mcp-Name` header, when present. */\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:115:    /** A notification without a body claim or a modern protocol-version header. */\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:131:     * `initialize` body's `protocolVersion`, or the `MCP-Protocol-Version`\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:132:     * header). Used to echo `requested` when legacy serving is not configured.\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:151:           * The classification handed to the per-request transport and validated by\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:164:/** The named steps of the inbound validation ladder, in evaluation order. */\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:172:    | 'standard-header-validation'\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:174:    | 'param-header-validation';\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:206: * The error code emitted for header/body cross-check mismatches: the\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:207: * `MCP-Protocol-Version` header disagreeing with the body's envelope claim (or\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:208: * with the body's classification), and the `Mcp-Method` header disagreeing\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:213: * by the published conformance suite for header-validation failures. It has no\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:215: * wire vocabulary; the validation ladder is its only emitter.\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:220: * The validation ladder as data\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:223:/** One rung of the inbound validation ladder. */\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:244: * The inbound validation ladder, expressed as data rather than control flow.\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:249: * gate, the envelope requiredness check, and per-method params validation).\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:251: * pre-dispatch, on the validated envelope the classifier produced — see that\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:281:        conformance: ['server-stateless', 'http-header-validation', 'http-custom-header-server-validation'],\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:283:            'Body-primary era classification with the protocol-version header as a cross-check; a header/body disagreement is rejected ' +\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:294:            'A present envelope claim with a malformed envelope — and a missing envelope on a request whose protocol-version header ' +\n./packages/core-internal/src/shared/inboundClassification.ts:314:     