zation server URL of the target MCP server (used as `audience` in the token exchange request).\n./packages/client/src/client/crossAppAccess.ts:32:     * The resource identifier of the target MCP server (RFC 9728).\n./packages/client/src/client/crossAppAccess.ts:50:     * Optional: the IdP may register the MCP client as a public client. RFC 8693 does\n./packages/client/src/client/crossAppAccess.ts:57:     * Optional space-separated list of scopes to request for the target MCP server.\n./packages/client/src/client/crossAppAccess.ts:84:     * The JWT Authorization Grant (ID-JAG) that can be used to request an access token from the MCP server.\n./packages/client/src/client/crossAppAccess.ts:103: * exchanges an ID Token for a JWT Authorization Grant that can be used with the target MCP server.\n./packages/client/src/client/crossAppAccess.ts:121: * // Use result.jwtAuthGrant with the MCP server's authorization server\n./packages/client/src/client/crossAppAccess.ts:152:        headers: {\n./packages/client/src/client/crossAppAccess.ts:224: * Exchanges a JWT Authorization Grant for an access token at the MCP server's authorization server.\n./packages/client/src/client/crossAppAccess.ts:227: * uses the JWT Authorization Grant to obtain an access token from the MCP server.\n./packages/client/src/client/crossAppAccess.ts:233: * Defaults to `client_secret_basic` (HTTP Basic Authorization header), matching\n./packages/client/src/client/crossAppAccess.ts:247: * // Use tokens.access_token to access the MCP server\n./packages/client/src/client/crossAppAccess.ts:273:    const headers = new Headers({\n./packages/client/src/client/crossAppAccess.ts:277:    applyClientAuthentication(authMethod, { client_id: clientId, client_secret: clientSecret }, headers, params);\n./packages/client/src/client/crossAppAccess.ts:281:        headers,\n./test/integration/test/client/client.test.ts:157:    // so HTTP transports can keep sending the required mcp-protocol-version header.\n./test/integration/test/client/client.test.ts:1779:describe('outputSchema validation', () => {\n./test/integration/test/client/client.test.ts:1783:    test('should validate structuredContent against outputSchema', async () => {\n./test/integration/test/client/client.test.ts:1852:        // Call the tool - should validate successfully\n./test/integration/test/client/client.test.ts:1928:        // Call the tool - should throw validation error\n./test/integration/test/client/client.test.ts:2068:        // Call the tool - should work normally without validation\n./test/integration/test/client/client.test.ts:2076:    test('should handle complex JSON schema validation', async () => {\n./test/integration/test/client/client.test.ts:2164:        // Call the tool - should validate successfully\n./test/integration/test/client/client.test.ts:2175:    test('should fail validation with additional properties when not allowed', async () => {\n./test/integration/test/client/client.test.ts:2245:        // Call the tool - should throw validation error due to additional property\n./test/integration/test/client/client.test.ts:2306:describe('Client sampling validation with tools', () => {\n./test/integration/test/client/client.test.ts:2307:    test('should validate array content with tool_use when request includes tools', async () => {\n./test/integration/test/client/client.test.ts:2312:        // Handler returns array content with tool_use - should validate with CreateMessageResultWithToolsSchema\n./test/integration/test/client/client.test.ts:2334:    test('should validate single content when request includes tools', async () => {\n./test/integration/test/client/client.test.ts:2339:        // Handler returns single content (text) - should still validate with CreateMessageResultWithToolsSchema\n./test/integration/test/client/client.test.ts:2358:    test('should validate single content when request has no tools', async () => {\n./test/integration/test/client/client.test.ts:2363:        // Handler returns single content - should validate with CreateMessageResultSchema\n./test/integration/test/client/client.test.ts:2386:        // Handler returns array content - should fail validation with CreateMessageResultSchema\n./test/integration/test/client/client.test.ts:2404:    test('should validate array content when request includes toolChoice', async () => {\n./packages/middleware/hono/src/middleware/originValidation.ts:1:import { localhostAllowedOrigins, validateOriginHeader } from '@modelcontextprotocol/server';\n./packages/middleware/hono/src/middleware/originValidation.ts:5: * Hono middleware for Origin header validation.\n./packages/middleware/hono/src/middleware/originValidation.ts:6: * Validates the `Origin` header hostname (port-agnostic) against an allowed list.\n./packages/middleware/hono/src/middleware/originValidation.ts:8: * Requests without an `Origin` header pass (non-browser MCP clients do not send\n./packages/middleware/hono/src/middleware/originValidation.ts:14:        const result = validateOriginHeader(c.req.header('origin'), allowedOriginHostnames);\n./packages/middleware/hono/src/middleware/originValidation.ts:33: * Convenience middleware for localhost Origin validation.\n./packages/middleware/hono/test/hono.test.ts:14:        const bad = await app.request('http://localhost/health', { headers: { Host: 'evil.com:3000' } });\n./packages/middleware/hono/test/hono.test.ts:26:        const good = await app.request('http://localhost/health', { headers: { Host: 'localhost:3000' } });\n./packages/middleware/hono/test/hono.test.ts:35:        const bad = await app.request('http://localhost/health', { headers: { Host: 'evil.com:3000' } });\n./packages/middleware/hono/test/hono.test.ts:38:        const good = await app.request('http://localhost/health', { headers: { Host: 'localhost:3000' } });\n./packages/middleware/hono/test/hono.test.ts:49:        const bad = await app.request('http://localhost/health', { headers: { Host: 'evil.com:3000' } });\n./packages/middleware/hono/test/hono.test.ts:52:        const good = await app.request('http://localhost/health', { headers: { Host: 'myapp.local:3000' } });\n./packages/middleware/hono/test/hono.test.ts:56:    test('createMcpHonoApp does not apply host validation for 0.0.0.0 without allowedHosts', async () => {\n./packages/middleware/hono/test/hono.test.ts:63:        const res = await app.request('http://localhost/health', { headers: { Host: 'evil.com:3000' } });\n./packages/middleware/hono/test/hono.test.ts:73:            headers: { Host: 'localhost:3000', 'content-type': 'application/json' },\n./packages/middleware/hono/test/hono.test.ts:86:            headers: { Host: 'localhost:3000', 'content-type': 'application/json' },\n./packages/middleware/hono/test/hono.test.ts:101:            headers: { Host: 'localhost:3000', 'content-type': 'text/plain; a=application/json' },\n./packages/middleware/hono/test/hono.test.ts:114:            headers: { Host: 'localhost:3000', 'content-type': 'application/json; charset=utf-8' },\n./packages/middleware/hono/test/hono.test.ts:131:            headers: { Host: 'localhost:3000', 'content-type': 'application/json' },\n./test/integration/test/client/versionNegotiation.test.ts:7: *   \"Unsupported protocol version\" literal (version header checked, no session),\n./test/integration/test/client/versionNegotiation.test.ts:12: * byte-identical to a plain legacy client's, zero 2026 headers), the typed\n./test/integration/test/client/versionNegotiation.test.ts:30:/** A fetch wrapper recording every request our client puts on the wire (URL, headers, body) and the raw response (status, body). */\n./test/integration/test/client/versionNegotiation.test.ts:34:        headers: Record<string, string>;\n./test/integration/test/client/versionNegotiation.test.ts:40:        const headers: Record<string, string> = {};\n./test/integration/test/client/versionNegotiation.test.ts:41:        for (const [key, value] of new Headers(init?.headers).entries()) {\n./test/integration/test/client/versionNegotiation.test.ts:42:            headers[key.toLowerCase()] = value;\n./test/integration/test/client/versionNegotiation.test.ts:49:            headers,\n./test/integration/test/client/versionNegotiation.test.ts:101:        // First contact: the probe POST (body-derived 2026 headers).\n./test/integration/test/client/versionNegotiation.test.ts:103:        expect(probe.headers['mcp-protocol-version']).toBe('2026-07-28');\n./test/integration/test/client/versionNegotiation.test.ts:104:        expect(probe.headers['mcp-method']).toBe('server/discover');\n./test/integration/test/client/versionNegotiation.test.ts:115:        // Fallback hygiene: ZERO 2026 headers on every post-probe request.\n./test/integration/test/client/versionNegotiation.test.ts:117:            expect(call.headers['mcp-method']).toBeUndefined();\n./test/integration/test/client/versionNegotiation.test.ts:118:            expect(call.headers['mcp-name']).toBeUndefined();\n./test/integration/test/client/versionNegotiation.test.ts:119:            const version = call.headers['mcp-protocol-version'];\n./test/integration/test/client/versionNegotiation.test.ts:141:        // POSTs before ever looking at the version header.\n./test/integration/test/client/versionNegotiation.test.ts:170:        // and the same headers (no clearing artifacts, no extras).\n./test/integration/test/client/versionNegotiation.test.ts:176:            expect(autoPosts[i]!.headers).toEqual(plainPost!.headers);\n./test/integration/test/client/versionNegotiation.test.ts:177:            for (const header of NEGOTIATION_HEADERS) {\n./test/integration/test/client/versionNegotiation.test.ts:178:                if (header === 'mcp-protocol-version') continue; // legacy value allowed post-initialize\n./test/integration/test/client/versionNegotiation.test.ts:179:                expect(autoPosts[i]!.headers[header]).toBeUndefined();\n./test/integration/test/client/versionNegotiation.test.ts:220:        expect(posts.every(c => c.headers['mcp-method'] === 'server/discover')).toBe(true);\n./packages/middleware/hono/src/middleware/hostHeaderValidation.ts:1:import { localhostAllowedHostnames, validateHostHeader } from '@modelcontextprotocol/server';\n./packages/middleware/hono/src/middleware/hostHeaderValidation.ts:6: * Validates `Host` header hostname (port-agnostic) against an allowed list.\n./packages/middleware/hono/src/middleware/hostHeaderValidation.ts:10:        const result = validateHostHeader(c.req.header('host'), allowedHostnames);\n./packages/server/README.md:3:The MCP (Model Context Protocol) TypeScript server SDK. Build MCP servers that expose tools, resources, and prompts.\n./packages/server/README.md:11:> This is **v2** of the MCP TypeScript SDK. It replaces the monolithic `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` package from v1. See the **[migration guide](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/blob/main/docs/migration/upgrade-to-v2.md)** if you're coming from v1.\n./packages/server/README.md:30:- **[MCP specification](https://modelcontextprotocol.io)**\n./test/integration/test/server.test.ts:1019:test('should validate elicitation response against requested schema', async () => {\n./test/integration/test/server.test.ts:1167:test('should allow elicitation reject and cancel without validation', async () => {\n./test/integration/test/server.test.ts:1214:    // Test reject - should not validate\n./test/integration/test/server.test.ts:1225:    // Test cancel - should not validate\n./test/integration/test/server.test.ts:1488:describe('createMessage validation', () => {\n./test/integration/test/server.test.ts:1743:    test('should validate tool_use/tool_result even without tools in current request', async () => {\n./test/integration/test/server.test.ts:1865:        // Mock client returns text content (tool_use schema validation is tested in types.test.ts)\n./test/integration/test/server.test.ts:1966:        const app = createMcpExpressApp({ host: '0.0.0.0' }); // Disable host validation for this test\n./test/integration/test/server.test.ts:1977:    test('should reject requests with invalid Host header by default', async () => {\n./test/integration/test/server.test.ts:1996:    test('should allow requests with localhost Host header', async () => {\n./test/integration/test/server.test.ts:2008:    test('should allow requests with 127.0.0.1 Host header', async () => {\n./test/integration/test/server.test.ts:2020:    test('should not apply host validation when host is 0.0.0.0', async () => {\n./test/integration/test/server.test.ts:2033:    test('should apply host validation when host is explicitly localhost', async () => {\n./test/integration/test/server.test.ts:2045:    test('should allow requests with IPv6 localhost Host header', async () => {\n./test/integration/test/server.test.ts:2057:    test('should apply host validation when host is ::1 (IPv6 localhost)', async () => {\n./test/integration/test/server.test.ts:2103:    test('should override default localhost validation when allowedHosts is provided', async () => {\n./packages/client/src/client/versionNegotiation.ts:10: * (probe headers derive from the probe message body) and is set exactly once\n./packages/middleware/README.md:3:The packages in `packages/middleware/*` are **thin integration layers** that help you expose an MCP server in a specific runtime, platform, or web framework.\n./packages/middleware/README.md:5:They intentionally **do not** add new MCP features or “business logic”. MCP functionality (tools, resources, prompts, transports, auth primitives, etc.) lives in `@modelcontextprotocol/server` (and other core packages). Middleware packages should primarily:\n./packages/middleware/README.md:13:- `@modelcontextprotocol/express` — Express helpers (app defaults + Host header validation for DNS rebinding protection).\n./packages/middleware/README.md:14:- `@modelcontextprotocol/hono` — Hono helpers (app defaults + JSON body parsing hook + Host header validation).\n./packages/middleware/README.md:21:- `@modelcontextprotocol/server` for the MCP server implementation\n./packages/server/CHANGELOG.md:20:- [#2402](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/pull/2402) [`a400259`](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/commit/a4002596b914c675d17ac22471d1287976dbb52a) Thanks [@felixweinberger](https://github.com/felixweinberger)! - First beta release of SDK v2 with support for the MCP 2026-07-28 specification\n./packages/server/CHANGELOG.md:30:    - `resultType` is no longer modeled by any neutral wire schema: `EmptyResultSchema` (strict) now rejects `{resultType}` bodies; on 2025-era connections a foreign `resultType` is stripped before validation instead of rejected; the member exists only inside the 2026-era codec, which requires it.\n./packages/server/CHANGELOG.md:32:    - Custom (3-arg) handlers now receive `_meta` minus the reserved envelope keys instead of having it deleted before params validation.\n./packages/server/CHANGELOG.md:64:- [#2286](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/pull/2286) [`1823aae`](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/commit/1823aae891837ebb5e3db885ec635f9efefd5771) Thanks [@felixweinberger](https://github.com/felixweinberger)! - SEP-1613 / SEP-2106 (JSON Schema 2020-12 posture): the Node default JSON Schema validator is now `Ajv2020` (true draft 2020-12) instead of the draft-07 `Ajv` class — `$defs`/`prefixItems`/`unevaluatedProperties`/`dependentRequired` are now enforced where they were previously silently ignored; to opt back, construct the draft-07 instance with the v1 defaults — `const ajv = new Ajv({ strict: false, validateFormats: true, validateSchema: false, allErrors: true }); addFormats(ajv);` — and pass `new AjvJsonSchemaValidator(ajv)`. Schemas declaring a `$schema` other than 2020-12 are rejected with a clear error rather than mis-validating. `outputSchema` may now have a non-object root and `CallToolResult.structuredContent` is widened to `unknown` (a deliberate source-level break for typed consumers — see the migration guide for the narrowing pattern). Toward 2025-era clients McpServer wraps a non-object `outputSchema` (and the matching `structuredContent`) in a `{result: …}` envelope so the tool stays callable, with same-document `$ref`/`$dynamicRef` pointers rewritten to keep resolving — low-level `Server` users (those bypassing `McpServer` and registering a `tools/call` handler directly) get the same wrap by routing the result through the new `Server.projectCallToolResult(result, advertisedOutputSchema)`. Independently, on every era (the SEP's MUST applies regardless of client version), McpServer auto-appends a `TextContent` JSON serialisation when a handler returns non-object `structuredContent` without its own text block. The `structuredContent` presence check is `!== undefined` (not falsy) on both sides. Thanks @mattzcarey (#2249).\n./packages/server/CHANGELOG.md:70:- [#2286](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/pull/2286) [`1823aae`](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/commit/1823aae891837ebb5e3db885ec635f9efefd5771) Thanks [@felixweinberger](https://github.com/felixweinberger)! - Results of the cacheable 2026-07-28 operations (`tools/list`, `prompts/list`, `resources/list`, `resources/templates/list`, `resources/read`, `server/discover`) now always carry the revision's required `ttlMs`/`cacheScope` fields when served on that revision, defaulting to `ttlMs: 0` / `cacheScope: 'private'`. Servers can configure the emitted values with the new `ServerOptions.cacheHints` option (per operation) and the new `cacheHint` member of the `registerResource` config (per resource); resolution is per field, most specific author first: cache fields returned by a handler win over the per-resource hint, which wins over the per-operation hint, and configured hints are validated at construction/registration time (`RangeError` on invalid values). Responses on 2025-era connections are unchanged and never carry these fields. Note for untyped callers: `registerResource` now interprets a `cacheHint` key in its config object — it is validated and kept out of the resource's list metadata, where it was previously passed through as ordinary metadata.\n./packages/server/CHANGELOG.md:72:- [#2286](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/pull/2286) [`1823aae`](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/commit/1823aae891837ebb5e3db885ec635f9efefd5771) Thanks [@felixweinberger](https://github.com/felixweinberger)! - Add `SdkErrorCode.MethodNotSupportedByProtocolVersion`: a typed local error raised before anything reaches the transport when a spec method is sent toward a peer whose negotiated protocol version's wire era does not define it (for example `tasks/get` toward a 2026-07-28 peer). The protocol layer now resolves a per-era wire codec from the connection's negotiated protocol version (instance state on `Client`/`Server`, with the legacy era as the pre-negotiation default) and resolves per-method schemas at dispatch time instead of registration time; an edge classification on an inbound message is validated against that instance era, and a mismatch is rejected as an entry/routing error. Behavior on existing (2025-era) connections is unchanged.\n./packages/server/CHANGELOG.md:77:  incomplete modern claims are NOT legacy and must be routed to the modern handler, which answers them with the documented validation errors. The predicate classifies a clone, so the routed request body stays readable. `legacyStatelessFallback` remains exported as a\n./packages/server/CHANGELOG.md:85:  always served over SSE. The entry performs no Origin/Host validation (use the middleware packages) and no token verification — `authInfo` is pass-through and never derived from request headers.\n./packages/server/CHANGELOG.md:87:- [#2381](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/pull/2381) [`f0bf785`](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/commit/f0bf785239af67f0cfce24575fec9c1193deaff1) Thanks [@felixweinberger](https://github.com/felixweinberger)! - Serve `input_required` handlers on 2025-era connections: the legacy shim (on by default) converts each embedded request of an `input_required` return into a real server→client request (`elicitation/create`, `sampling/createMessage`, `roots/list`) over the live session — stamped with the originating request's id for stream association — and re-enters the handler with the collected `inputResponses` until a final result. Handlers are written once in the 2026 `inputRequired(...)` style and serve both eras; the previous loud `-32603` failure remains available via `ServerOptions.inputRequired.legacyShim: false`. Knobs: `inputRequired.maxRounds` (default 8) and `inputRequired.roundTimeoutMs` (default 600 000 ms per leg; legs carry a progressToken so a client reporting progress mid-leg resets the leg timeout). Semantics mirror the modern client driver exactly: per-round replaced `inputResponses`, byte-exact `requestState` echo with the verify hook running every round, paced requestState-only rounds, and elicitation accepted content passed through UNVALIDATED (the handler validates via the schema-aware `acceptedContent` overload, exactly as on the 2026 era). URL-mode legs synthesize the `elicitationId` the 2025-11-25 wire requires. Failures surface per family (`tools/call` → `isError` tool result; `prompts/get` / `resources/read` → JSON-RPC error); stateless legacy HTTP degrades to a clean capability refusal; the shim emits no progress of its own (the originating progressToken is the handler's single must-increase stream — the shim never adds a second author to it).\n./packages/server/CHANGELOG.md:91:    New typed readers for `inputResponses`, exported from `@modelcontextprotocol/server`: a schema-aware `acceptedContent(responses, key, schema)` overload (validates untrusted accepted content against any synchronous Standard Schema) and `inputResponse(responses, key)` (discriminated `missing | elicit | sampling | roots` view, for decline/cancel detection and the non-elicitation kinds). Content conveniences like text extraction stay in application code as one-liners over the discriminated view.\n./packages/server/CHANGELOG.md:96:  to request additional client input in-band; the structured-content requirement and the tools/call result-schema validation are skipped for that return, the encode seam emits it as `resultType: 'input_required'`, and the handler reads the responses on re-entry from\n./packages/server/CHANGELOG.md:103:- [#2286](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/pull/2286) [`1823aae`](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/commit/1823aae891837ebb5e3db885ec635f9efefd5771) Thanks [@felixweinberger](https://github.com/felixweinberger)! - Add Origin header validation alongside the existing Host header validation. The server package gains framework-agnostic helpers (`validateOriginHeader`, `localhostAllowedOrigins`, `originValidationResponse`); the Express, Hono and Fastify adapters gain `originValidation` /\n./packages/server/CHANGELOG.md:104:  `localhostOriginValidation` middleware and a new `allowedOrigins` option on their app factories, which now arm Origin validation by default for localhost-class binds (mirroring the Host validation ladder; the 0.0.0.0-without-allowlist warning is unchanged). Requests\n./packages/server/CHANGELOG.md:105:  without an `Origin` header pass — non-browser MCP clients are unaffected — while a present `Origin` that is not allowed or cannot be parsed (including the opaque `null` origin) is rejected with `403`. The Node adapter ships `hostHeaderValidation` / `originValidation`\n./packages/server/CHANGELOG.md:106:  request guards for plain `node:http` servers, which previously had no validation helpers.\n./packages/server/CHANGELOG.md:108:- [#2286](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/pull/2286) [`1823aae`](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/commit/1823aae891837ebb5e3db885ec635f9efefd5771) Thanks [@felixweinberger](https://github.com/felixweinberger)! - SEP-2243 `Mcp-Param-*` server-side validation (protocol revision 2026-07-28). On the modern (2026-07-28) serving path, `createMcpHandler` now validates `Mcp-Param-{Name}` headers against the named tool's `x-mcp-header` declarations and the body `arguments` before dispatch: a missing header for a present body value, a header that decodes to a different value than the body, or an invalid `=?base64?…?=` sentinel is rejected with `400 Bad Request` and JSON-RPC `-32020` (`HeaderMismatch`) — the same shape the existing standard-header cross-checks emit. A `null`/absent body value passes regardless of any header (the spec's \"server MUST NOT expect the header\" rows). `McpServer.registerTool` now warns at registration time when an `x-mcp-header` declaration violates the spec's constraints. The 2025-era serving paths and the low-level `Server` factory shape are unchanged.\n./packages/server/CHANGELOG.md:110:- [#2286](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/pull/2286) [`1823aae`](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/commit/1823aae891837ebb5e3db885ec635f9efefd5771) Thanks [@felixweinberger](https://github.com/felixweinberger)! - SEP-2243 standard-header server-side validation (protocol revision 2026-07-28). On the modern (2026-07-28) serving path, `createMcpHandler` now enforces the required `Mcp-Method` and `Mcp-Name` standard request headers in addition to the existing `MCP-Protocol-Version` and `Mcp-Method` cross-checks: a modern request without an `Mcp-Method` header, a `tools/call` / `prompts/get` / `resources/read` request without an `Mcp-Name` header, an `Mcp-Name` header carrying an invalid `=?base64?…?=` sentinel, and an `Mcp-Name` header whose (decoded) value disagrees with the body's `params.name` / `params.uri` are all rejected with `400 Bad Request` and JSON-RPC `-32020` (`HeaderMismatch`). The 2025-era serving paths are unchanged.\n./packages/server/CHANGELOG.md:113:    - `@modelcontextprotocol/server`: the `mcpNameHeader` field on `InboundHttpRequest`, and the `'standard-header-validation'` member of `InboundValidationRung` (with `client-capabilities` / `param-header-validation` renumbered).\n./packages/server/CHANGELOG.md:147:- [#2286](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/pull/2286) [`1823aae`](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/commit/1823aae891837ebb5e3db885ec635f9efefd5771) Thanks [@felixweinberger](https://github.com/felixweinberger)! - Deprecate `Server.getClientCapabilities()`, `Server.getClientVersion()` and `Server.getNegotiatedProtocolVersion()` in favor of the per-request handler context: on 2026-07-28 requests the validated `_meta` envelope carries the client's identity (`ctx.mcpReq.envelope`),\n./packages/server/CHANGELOG.md:154:- [#2286](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/pull/2286) [`1823aae`](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/commit/1823aae891837ebb5e3db885ec635f9efefd5771) Thanks [@felixweinberger](https://github.com/felixweinberger)! - Pin the modern (2026-07-28) HTTP serving path's rejection codes to the assignments the published conformance suite asserts: a header/body cross-check mismatch (`MCP-Protocol-Version` or `Mcp-Method` disagreeing with the request body) is now rejected with `-32020` (HeaderMismatch), and a request whose protocol-version header names a modern revision but whose body is missing the `_meta` envelope (or its required protocol-version key) is rejected with `-32602` invalid params naming the missing key(s). Both keep HTTP 400. These cells previously emitted a provisional `-32004` while the upstream error-code discussion was open. The envelope-less rejection on a modern-only endpoint (`-32022` with the supported-versions list), the 2025-era serving paths, and the client-side probe handling are unchanged.\n./packages/server/CHANGELOG.md:165:    - The error code `-32001` emitted for HTTP header/body mismatches is now defined by the draft schema (`HEADER_MISMATCH`); the emitted behavior is unchanged (documentation only).\n./packages/server/CHANGELOG.md:188:    Response result-schema validation failure now rejects with `SdkError(InvalidResult)` instead of a raw `ZodError`. Adds `SdkErrorCode.InvalidResult`.\n./packages/server/CHANGELOG.md:192:  This closes the gap with the MCP spec, which allows `icons` on tools, resources, resource templates, and prompts.\n./packages/server/CHANGELOG.md:195:  `IdJagTokenExchangeResponse` type to the public API and register its schema as an MCP spec type. `IdJagTokenExchangeResponse` is now exported from `@modelcontextprotocol/client` and `@modelcontextprotocol/server`, `'IdJagTokenExchangeResponse'` joins the `SpecTypeName` union,\n./packages/server/CHANGELOG.md:196:  and `isSpecType.IdJagTokenExchangeResponse(value)` / `specTypeSchemas.IdJagTokenExchangeResponse` validate it by name — matching how the OAuth/OpenID auth schemas are already exposed. The Zod schema itself, `IdJagTokenExchangeResponseSchema`, is available from\n./packages/server/CHANGELOG.md:204:  `specTypeSchemas` records for runtime validation of any MCP spec type by name. `isSpecType.ContentBlock(value)` is a type predicate; `specTypeSchemas.ContentBlock` is a `StandardSchemaV1Sync<ContentBlock>` validator — `validate()` returns the result synchronously. Guards are\n./packages/server/CHANGELOG.md:272:    The `jsonSchemaValidator` interface remains the public extension point for replacing validation entirely with a custom implementation.\n./packages/server/CHANGELOG.md:294:  unknown tools and resources per MCP spec.\n./packages/server/CHANGELOG.md:314:    `StandardSchemaWithJSON` (requires both `~standard.validate` and `~standard.jsonSchema`) instead of the Zod-specific `AnySchema` type.\n./packages/server/CHANGELOG.md:347:    - Removed unused exports from `@modelcontextprotocol/core-internal`: `SchemaInput`, `schemaToJson`, `parseSchemaAsync`, `getSchemaShape`, `getSchemaDescription`, `isOptionalSchema`, `unwrapOptionalSchema`. Use the new `standardSchemaToJsonSchema` and `validateStandardSchema`\n./packages/server/CHANGELOG.md:373:  silently swallowed by adding missing `onerror` callback invocations before all `createJsonErrorResponse` calls in `WebStandardStreamableHTTPServerTransport`. This ensures errors like parse failures, invalid headers, and session validation errors are properly reported via the\n./packages/server/src/server/serveStdio.ts:74:    validateEnvelopeMeta\n./packages/server/src/server/serveStdio.ts:302: * is validated (never silently ignored); a claim-less message is 2025-era\n./packages/server/src/server/serveStdio.ts:303: * traffic. There is no header layer on stdio, so the body is the only signal.\n./packages/server/src/server/serveStdio.ts:320:    // A present claim is validated, never silently ignored — a malformed\n./packages/server/src/server/serveStdio.ts:324:    const issues = meta === undefined ? [] : validateEnvelopeMeta(meta);\n./packages/server/src/server/serveStdio.ts:360: * Serves MCP over stdio from a server factory, owning the era decision for\n./packages/server/src/server/serveStdio.ts:442:            // recognized the method — so envelope validation is the first\n./packages/server/src/server/serveStdio.ts:448:                ? (meta === undefined ? [] : validateEnvelopeMeta(meta))[0]\n./packages/server-legacy/src/auth/middleware/bearerAuth.ts:19:     * Optional resource metadata URL to include in WWW-Authenticate header.\n./packages/server-legacy/src/auth/middleware/bearerAuth.ts:27:         * Information about the validated access token, if the `requireBearerAuth` middleware was used.\n./packages/server-legacy/src/auth/middleware/bearerAuth.ts:34: * Middleware that requires a valid Bearer token in the Authorization header.\n./packages/server-legacy/src/auth/middleware/bearerAuth.ts:36: * This will validate 